        <p>The rush to the metropolitan theatree, music halls end other places of amusement, on Monday night vria• without a previous parsliel, and when isle considered that there is now • theatre in every part of the suburbs, the number of persons wiling re- creation of this kind at the present season must be enormously on the increase. I have already men- Honed that the number of pantomime. in London this Christmas exc.++ a couple of dogen, and this , leases not of the reckoning the hunt of theatres and halla which do not produce that form of entertain- ment. There was probably not a single house at which crowds of people were not turned away for want even of stencliog room, and I myself saw announcements at the doors of some theatres, holf an hour belore the performance began, stating that no more space wee available. At Barnum's there were two performances, and persona who had botiked their team. Could not even net into the building for the crowd which wan struggling out- side for unreserved pieces. Those who succeeded wpre not iiisappoisited in the multitude of wonders they saw, which are too numerous, to be overtaken in half a dozen inspections. Yet Barnum's had only 23,000 vi•it ore, who exhausted the Lill capacity of the how.. while the World's Pair at the Agricul- tural Hall had BS 000, nod Madame Tauentufn 35,030. This given sew indication of how myriads of tandoners spent Boxing Day and evening.</p>
        <p>It haw been remarked by many Caterers for chit- drone tots for Chriorma. this year that there has been a decided run on t hose leaden soldiers which'l ire dressed in Highland co 4 time. This no doubt is owing te tho gallant ronduct of the Gordon High- lander. at Gomel. It is a pity, however, that the • Gordon plaid in not better known ; most of the leaden Highlanders are painted in the Rob Roy</p>
        <p>Lord Mansfisht,the oldest living peer of the realm is reported to born feeble health. Main ninety-two peers of age. Lord Ment.lield and Mr. Gladstone are the solo televise. of Sir Robert Peel'. Administra- tion of 1834-5, in which they were both Lords of the Treasury. Lord Mansfield is the senior member of the Larltou Club, to which be was elected in 1832 and he is also the nenior knight of the Thistle. ha.- . ing received his blue ribbon in 181.3 from Ai. Robert Peel. He ims • member of the Rouse of Com- mons (as Lord Stormont) from 1530 until he suc- ceeded to rho peerage in 1840. Scone Palace. his place in Perthshiro. is one of the most interesting country neva in Great Britain.</p>
        <p>Considerable ettantion has been excited among influential Americans here by a movement for th• formation of an Anglo-American Association, bay- ing for its main object the cultivation of peace and goodwill betwern Kngland and America. There see many who think that a society for this purpose is superfluow, and that peace and goodwill between the two countries can be sufficiently cultivated under existing circumstances by private individuals and by private men. But there are others who seem to anticipate that the formal enrolment of members of an association on both tides of the At- lantic, particularly if Vies arc persons of influence with their ropeetivo countrymen, might tend to happy results. At any rate it is urged that the movement cannot do harm to the intareata of either nation.</p>
