        <p>, Mr. H. W. Wilton, in his estimate of the situation in the Far East, which appears in one of the morn- ing papers, expressed his clear conviction not only that the slicing up of China is an old standing , arrangement bet ween three Continental Powers,but Ina it is the duty of England to look after iteelf without fennel time. But he points out also that , our fleet is not strong enough in t he Yellow Sel,the combined fleets of Busse., France, end Germany representing 14,000 One more tons the British fleet. In gun power the Russians alone are nearly equal to or; and his moral from this lea is that our squadron should be promptly reinforced. He ed- ema the seizure of Chuman, or any hotter position, without hexitation or delay. if. disposes once for all of the notion that we can turn Germany out ot %inc.-Chen Bay when that place is ours fortified, and he le euepicions, moreover, that _part of the arrangement between the three Continght al Powers Ile to attack Japan before ite feet is strengthened by the numereusJapanese ships now bring built in European dockyards. Any such project, he main- tains, and justly, most he vetoed by England. It would never be attempted in face of even the • Morellt suspicion that a common understanding ex- irted between England and Japan, and if such an understanding would limit the possibility of the enticipated scramble for Chins the sooner it is made manifest the better:</p>
        <p>It la interesting to note (ho immensely increased popularity of carol-singing at Christmas time. Keen the unbending 8:104,111.11 of the Abbey ser- vice ban thawed a little to make room for it, the enthral at the Brat eveneong of the featieal being *heaved in favour of a selection of carols. At St. Paul's caro.s hate been acclimatised for years, and Saturday'm celebrati,n was conception. The music at the Cathedrel was unusually beautiful, Schubert's Base in B fiat being sung at the midday celebration of the Communion service. The cu. monary Handelian anthems were Oren. and the Cathedral organ broke the silence which has been unposed upon riot late byte repsoil and enlarge- ment it is undergoing at the hands of Mr. Willis.</p>
        <p>The compliments of the RelL•011 come, ems the " Doily Chronicle," thick upon ov. One day we hemd of a whole family burnt to death in One room. The next dm we have two dew he through the fog •in Si lwall Dock, one of a lighterman who vme ; thrown into the water by running into another • barge, the other of a docker who fell into dm basin for want of shflLient protection to the gangway of the ship. In this cam the coroner cheeringly re- marked upon the great imptoverneet Once the old days whet he mod to have twenty loquesta I. • foggy week, Next COlma two women found in the Leant a place wtere the towing path ieee danger- ous that • boatman stated he had taken thirty-nix bodies frets the water there end leaved twenty- nine lives in a year. One of the women was a widow earning three shillings a week or some- timen lour," and paying a weekly rent of four -billings out of that new. Theo we have the com- paratively imignificant mem of • man miming for three weeks and suddenly found in Duckett's Canal, lunknown woman No. I found in Grand Surrey • Canal," age about fifty, very poor " ; unknown I woman No. 2 found in negent'v Canal," latchkey • and one farthing in her pocket "f unknown woman I No.) found in an area in Albany-street " with a I small sum of money and an umbrella." Lastly in , Liverpool we hem a poor half-drunken, half- paralysed creature on Chrietma. Wet malting hin clothes with turpentine and par.filo, and burning himself to death because "ho wan tired of lifr." When hie wife interfered, lie threw her out of doors ' and burnt alone. It was s strange Christinae tree to light. a God rest you, merry gentlemen."</p>
