        <p>Mrs. Margie, anxious to do something to assist in the reeovtring of the missing bride, remembered that Beryl had left artieles of needlework on sale at a ladies' repository and a bazaar in Oxford- street. Dummied seised upon this due and followed it up, but Beryl had not been to either place to inquire tiller toe results of her labour. A Rae more than at week allot Beryl's dimppearance, be.- eat alone to hlss elimnbers in Chancery-lane. The hour was late afternoon, approaching evening. He had been hard at work since looming. He had hoard of a mysterious young girl at Clapham, whiwe description tallied with that of BeryL He had accordingly visited Claphain, spent the day in searching for toe mysterious young lady, and had succeeded at last in finding her, but she had proved to he a young actress who was in hiding from het father, amd he hail returned discouraged and dis- heartened. Cuable to bear the immtionings of his fellow-lodgem in Wels-street, he had come to his chambers to spend rio hour or two in solitude. The janitor had made a cheery fire in his grata, and its red glow lighted up the dull room. The gone was carpeted. The walls were lined with books. There wan is couch at one side of the chim- ney, there were cany.c hairs, a reading and&amp; writing- desk. It woo it snug little bachelor's retreat. There was I% knuek upon his door. Before he could speak it swung open, tied Dane Conyers came sauntering in.</p>
        <p>Are 3:-.ou here, old fellow ?" asked Conyers, with seeming heartiness. "Oh, there you are? Don't rite, 1 Org. 1 just dropped in upon you fora few minutes. Lord Hawkhurst insisted upon my coming, although I was anxionu myself to see you. You are not looking well."</p>
        <p>"Help yourself in a chair, Conyers," said Dew mood. "la Mrs. Conyers well? Is Lord Hawk- buret in good health? '</p>
        <p>Cony.; wheeled up a second lounging-chair, and flung himself into it in a careless attitude. " Mrs. Conyere is well, thank you," he said, "1 think that Lord Hawkhuret is not very well. He does not seem quite himself lately. I haven't seen you since we were at Hawks' Cliff Castle together, Desmond. What changes • few months have wrought I" " Yes, indeed, what changes! You are married to Miss Windsor of Cragthorpe, and your prospects art most brilliant, I—"</p>
        <p>You are married, toe? I have heard from the earl your sad story, Desmond—how your bride was stricken with fever at the very altar, and wandered away from you in a fit of delirium. Accept my .rnese sympathy. lam very sorry for you, Des- mond."</p>
        <p>--H;:spoke with such a seeming of sincerity as to touch Desmond'. heart. The latter silently eO. tended his hand, and Conyers grasped it fervently. " It is a sad occurrence—very sad," said Conyers, sorrowfully. "Did you know her before you went to Hawks Cliff?"</p>
        <p>Desm.dMi.Ahis.ll.4,ligifyigante.</p>
        <p>"I understand that she 'lva;isv•VidZw "Yen ; and only seventeen years of age," said Desmond. She lost hsr hus'oan hy an aceidsnt, niter a month's marriage. He lost his life in an ascent of one of the Swiss Alps. Her first marriage was a sad affair altogether. We never talked of it. But I do know that her first husband was a villian. She never wore a mourning dress for him, and Inns sure she did not mourn for him in her heart. Had °iron -.stances been different—had she not been no ill-paid awl hard-worked—we should have dole, red our marriage for a year; but even that, small mark of respect, for his memory was not deserved by him."</p>
        <p>He must have heen villion I" said Conyers. "Poor young ally!l SIM was well rid of hint. What stops have you taken tolled her!" Conyers's exaltation was carefully concea'ed in his own heart, lie saw that Desmond lied not the faiuteet anspicion that "Vane Convoy" wns an other than his present guest. He seemed to him- self to ainlinsh—to lie hidden end unsus- pected behind his screen of fair end honest seeming. Only yesterday lie had visited Beryl at Bayswater, f and to-day he was playing the sympathizing! friend to the man who would give all he owned to knew Beryl's wlmreatouto "I have employed • detective," said Desmond, "trot he is not the hest on the force and I have little faith in him. The detective I desired to employ is up north engaged in working up a ease. depend most of all on my own exertions and on Sherwald."</p>
        <p>• Sherwald I Not Lord Hawkhorst's solicitor?"</p>
        <p>"Y. By i.lie.direQe-os and wishes of the earl, Mr. Sherwidal is woiking with and for .no. Ho • very sagaciou• man, C0ny...." "Yea," said Conyere, absently. "So Sherwald is working with you. It'. odd—deuced odd, you know. I don't like Sherwald."</p>
        <p>Ho is very clever." Conyers moved uneasily. The suggestion of Sherwald's cleverness was not pleasant to hum " Have you issued any handbills ?" he inquired. "Rave you put up any placarde, or anything of tbatwort . . _</p>
        <p>Desmond, his fair face flushing. "We 0..u1d not do that. There was a possibility, you see, Conyers, that she—Mrs. Desmond—was not delitioue, alter all. and that she is liviog quietly somewhere, and to advertise her publicly none would advertise • strayed horse would not do at all."</p>
        <p>v.. v.v... "You look tired out My carriage ie below let me take you to your lodging.. I don't like to leave you moping here. Come, Deetnond. You ertay find a letter there, you know." The feeling which Deemond had experienced of Beryl's proximity to him returned volth renewed form. Conyers's words seemed arprophecy of good. He arose, put on hie great coat, and they descended the stain together. Conyers set Desmond down in NVellsottreet, and drove on to Park lane. .</p>
        <p>Desmond went up to his own rooms and lighted his lamp. There was no letter for him on the table. He locked his door, turned down his lighi, and not down at his window, gaxing down into tho street. No one came to disturb him, his entnivee into the house haring been unnoticed, thanks to his latuh.key. The dull twilight deepened into night. The hours wore on, and still Desmond did not ear. Bet suddenly he drew a quick, sharp breath, nod leaned forward, his eyes glowing, his face whiten- ing. Out from the uhulows between the gas-lampe on the opposite side of the street, a slender, girlish figure was moving slowly. She was dresised black, nod her face looked muffled. Her gem, wn. fixed upon Mrs. Margle's hou.—upon Deintiond's indOWN. Opposite the hon.o eke stood, eoutinu- ing to look, lie could not nee her face, but her at- titude was one of unutterable lungtog, of pro. • -</p>
        <p>Sonne one drew the parlour blinds in the houee over the way, and • sudden stream of light fell upon the slender figure in bliwk. At the same moment • fitful gust of wintry wind blew her veil back from her ram Wan and sorrowful, thin and clrey,L it woo still the beautiful young face of Denniond rm. down the stair. end out of the beim. Beryl now him voining, and flitted away Bk. • spirit She Sassed into Oxford•treet. VW. Be .ten tented the owner. eh* bed alt.</p>
