        <head>CHAPTER XXXVIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXVIII.</p>
        <p>'et .11 fortnigla ef.!..7 !inure disappearance</p>
        <p>Wells-street:'Lord,Hawkilu'rst sent :trinrempr</p>
        <p>. _ , summons to &amp;is solicitor to come to him a` Mr. Sherwald obeyed, arriving at Hawk burst House exalt nine o'clock in the evening, and before the earl had come up from the dining- toom.</p>
        <p>Sherwald was shown into the library as ueuaL The earl presently joined him. The solicitor' face expressed hie surprise at the change a few days had wronght in the appearance of his lord ship. And yet it wan hard to tell in what the change consisted. It woo only that Iris step teas languid when it had always been quick and ring mg ; only that the ruddinesn of hie visage had faded somewhat ; that his umaive features looked shrunken and pinched, and tli.,t km eyes seemed unnaturally keen and bright. 'Arc you ill, my Lord" tact only. " No. What, seneelene questions people ask said the earl, lretfully. "11l tact never ill. Be good enough to remember that, Slierwald. Why have you not been here within a week ? Do you expect me to hunt you up ? Sit down." She; weld obeyed, continuing to regard his em- ployer with a look of solbritude. "I have been very busy during the past week, any Lord," he explained. "I sent you a note nearly every day to. let you know how I NILS get- ting 011, but I had 00 news to bring. I have not made the draft of the new will yet—" . . .</p>
        <p>sni.l Sherwahl, invo-</p>
        <p>"Bother the new will There in :to hurry about that. There's time enough for wills during the next twenty years I want to know about Dee- mond. Hen he found his wife yet t"</p>
        <p>"No, my Lord." ",But I told you to look for her—to leave no matins untried to tiuil her." • " I have 'marched for her, hut have not yet been succeaaful," said Sheroald. "I shall keep up the search, my Lord." " Perhaps she's killed herself. Women are fools anyhow," growled the earl. "If she had remained with Desmond I ntibtlit have seen her and asked her • few questions. I almuld like to know how she came into poowasion oh toot necklace." o Probably her that husband gave it to her," suggested Sherwald. " Iler first husband Yes, yes, probably; but where did be get ii? That's the question. Fd given good deal to see this young woman, Sher- weld,' said the earl, restlessly. " I most see her." " We shall soon find her, my Lord, if abe'll alive. Desmond is devoting his life to the xearch for her, and I am doing the same," said Sherwald, inwardly convinced that the earl wiper:teal Conyers of being Beryl s first husband, and Oita accounting in his own mind for the earl's solicitude to find Beryl. " We shall leave no stone unturned in our search. By the way, my Lord, does Mr. Conyers know that the will ilevtsing all your property to him haa been destroyed ?" " Ceriainly not, He knew of the existence of the will, lint it is not necessary that Ile should know that it has ceased to exist. I shall make another will immediately, and he will lose little by the change." "It is still your purr.° then, my Lord, to make isle. Conyers your heir? " "Certainly. Why should I change my ex. Pressed purpose ? Its is honourable, has an unblemished name, and has never done anything to blush for. True, he has sown his crops of wild oats, but all young men do that. I can forgive prodigality, recklessness and folly in young men, hut I could never forgive wickedness and crime, nor that felly.which Helmond has committed, of finking himself to a woman who in no doubt as adventuress."</p>
        <p>Sherwald looked thoughtful and troubled. He zegarded the earl with a ,keen scrutiny, taking note of the change in his appearance, but he did not venture again to remark upon it. " I hardly think Desinond's wife is an adven- turess," he said, arousing himself. "Desmond thinks 'her an angel. This anxiety is wearing him out. He's a noble fellow, my Lord, although I own that he wse imprudent in marrying a woman whose history he did not fully know. But that fault is not common among young men in this day %MI generation, you must grant. As to the lady,l do not indorse Desmond's theory of fever, or hallu- cination, or what not."</p>
        <p>YOU don't What do you think, then?" "I think that ehe actually saw her first haw band, as 1 mid to you once before, my Lord." " A pretty state of affair. !" muttered the earl. "Desmond married to a woman who has another huaband living? Preposterous ! I wonder the woman don't kill henielf. Is she of loreigu birth 1" _</p>
        <p>--;:l‘fri, my Lord. She was educated ten Switzer. land, lint was horn in England." "Her name was Conroy, you mid r " Her first husband's name was Conroy. Her maiden nume was Star." " What A great agitation convulsed the earl's counten- num. Ilk lace flushed his eyes flamed. " What did you say her name west" he demanded. Star—Beryl Star. Desmond told me yester.</p>
        <p>•I Star Her name Star ! That attenuate— Yee ; I understand.. anderetand—" . . .</p>
        <p>Lord Ilawkhurst walked away to the window. Ile was bilent for some minutes. When he returned o the tireeitle, he was calm and quiet, but start. lingly pale, and his eyes still glowed and burned like lamps. Slicreald's conviction that his lordship knew something concerning Beryl's first husband, or sus. peat.' more, received tionfirmation in the earl's singular agitation. "I should like to me Desmond's wife," mid Lord Hawklitirst, county. "For his sake, we must scour LUILLIOLI—EugI.d even—to find her. The scandal must be kept hushed up, you understand. Ironl tired, Sherwald. I'm not equal to business this evening.'' He flung himmlf into a chair and threw back his head upon the cushions, his massive throat thrown into full relief. He looked tired and haggard. Sherwald's concern for him got the better of ids discretion.</p>
        <p>" My Lard, I wish you would see • physician,. he said. '• I don't think you are at all yourself—. Pshawl well enough. I hate a pack a meddling croakers!" growled the earl. " There— there You mean well enough, no doubt, Sher- vial&amp; but lam not ill. I'll take • run down to Hawke' Cliff lore few day. The stiff salt breeze. will met me up. Neety weather we've beau baying." The conversation drifted into other chautiels. An hour later the solicitor took him leave.</p>
        <p>He had staireely departed when Lord Hawk- hunt began to pace hie room a. a lion walks his cage; rhat mane !" he muttered. "This girl belong. to the tame family. Her posaession of the neck- lace proves that. This Beryl Star is William Star's aught,. No doubt he's married and hsa a large family. But how came the necklace in hie buds Can it be that after the lapee of year. I shall find noire to her Into I Is she alive or dead? I must see William Star. I will run down to his place to-morrow." Toward noon of the next day, the knocker was sounded, and the becalved and beplushed hall- porter opened the door. A young lady dreeeed in black, with a small parcel in her hand, wu stand- ing on the stone stem. The hall-porter admitted her into the marble.paved hall, and she asked to tee "Lady Hawkhurst." The young lady warn Beryl. The hall-porter smiled Lao he sawed her that there was no Lady Hawkhurst. "There must be some mietaket madam," he Wel. " My lmly have been dead for twenty years." "Then it must be her daughter I am 000. me," mid Beryl. "The address worn Hawkhurst Home, Bartle.. I call by a meal. My name is Miss Star." "Perhaps," suggested the mums% at is kb&amp; Conyers you veish to see." Before Beryl mold reply, OctesvM's maid, wile was peering over the balusters on the upper Oaks landing, called out, sharply : "If the young person has come to me my lady about mine embroidery, Butter% let her come up Pb,. is expected." Beryl iiiLl.4ll ecross the hall and up the Main The lady's maid received her at the landing and oonducted her op to the next floor, mhering her into Octacts's boudoir.. ' •</p>
        <p>Mr. Cunyere wes *lone. She wore • dressing- gown of 011 cAtihmere, ml.l • profusion ot scarlet eihbone. II er Idaek hair was heaped high upon her head in fasloonaole dishevelinenh Her hard black eyes gr w height at eight of Beryl, and she greeted her visitor with a gracious sisnle. '•1 rather expected you to.day,' she said, owe deseeedingly. •. Prey be igated. Mise-11Lise--"</p>
