        <p>ad Beryl, chair.covm. impeoljon. 1 The wervant May 1 .^</p>
        <p>" Mies Star," . /simply. "1 hoes 4,nished one of the 1, madam, and have Jroug ht it for your I inquired for you . Lady Hawkhurst. rant stmke of you as Mrs. Conyers, I think. I ask d that, if your name ?" said Oetavia, earl.sly.. " It's a eommou name enongla,ilp my husband. of very anew. cretin family. He will be the next Lord Hawk. buret."</p>
        <p>mew. "I suppose the name is common," said Beryl, who had linen cuisiongly pale. " Permit me to show you my work, madame. I designed the pattern myself, and have nude dr:twings of the remaining patterns, that you may see if you ince them." like unrolkil her parcel, displaying • square of white satin upon which was exqulsitelp embroi- dered a wreath of pale plot r..sen " How lovely !" cried Oetavia. " You have genius, Miss Star. I wish I were clever like you. My chairs will tie the envy of slimy friends. How beautifully those loses a- e shaded I never cow handsomer needlework even in Paris." Beryl wild tinned at these warm commenda- tions. She began to unroll hoe tiny parcel of drawin " Sitgs. down here at the fire, Mies Star," said Mrs. Conyerr. " Lieette, you may go." Beryl sat down at the corner of the hearth in a low Marquise chair, and exhibited her d.igns, Mrs. Conyers admired them all, and expressed her admiration in warm terms.</p>
        <p>"Von will make your fortune," she said. shall mania second set when these are done, and shall recommend you to all my friends. You ought to adopt. French name. As Madame Etoile you would receive double prices and double fame. You know what the world in, Mies Star. But I dare say you could not speak French." "01,, yes, madam,' said Beryl. "I wag educated in Switzerland, and speak French and German as fluently as I speak English." "Oh, indeed," said Octavio., congratulating her- self upon the ease with which she was ingratiating herself in Beryl's confidence. " I fancied I detec a foreign air about you, Miss Star. I was educated in Paris. You seem young to have left school, Have you experienced a reverse of fortune? Have 3'll rerents:erloln ieviinngt?h"e world, madam," said Beryl, with a quiet dignity that impressed even Octavm " I have no one to support and no one to support me. I suppose I was educated to become a gover- nese, boil am not able to find a situation, having noose to recommend me."</p>
        <p>" Embroidery like this must poy better thus teachitg. I should not think you would be obliged to work a great while to support yourself. ThaS beautiful face of yours ought to secure you a rich husband." ._ . ,</p>
        <p>Beryl flushed and drew herself up with hauteur. Ibe your pardon if my freedom has offended you,. said Octavio. lam awkward, perhaps, in paying compliment. I was interested in you, 1112.1 fancied I could read a whole remain in posse bleak dress and lonely position. / should have been pleased to be of service to you, but, as you inti- mate, we will confine ourselves to business. I will pay you for the chair-cover you have brought home. Ilere is the money</p>
        <p>She counted out the five sovereigns she hid agreed to pay. " I should like my hushand's opinions upon the remaining designs," she continued, smoothly. "He hes the tastes of no artist, and will admire your work, I no. sure. I will call him." Her limat throbbed fast with an evil exultation as slie wont through her dressing.room into that of</p>
        <p>Conyers. " Rollyn2" she said, nialiciously, "come into my boudoir. I have something lovely to show you." " All right," replied Conyers. "I'll be there is • minute."</p>
        <p>OctsWit. returned to BeryL "My lundwid is coming," she 811111 '1 do hope Ire Will admire your work, but of course he will like them if I do. He is perfectly devoted to me, thinks me an angel, and so on, hut that's a weak. seen of young husisiuda, I suppose." Beryl was silent. Octavia listened iinpatiently for Congress step. She was eager to solve the mystery of the relations minting between this beautiful girl and Rollys Conyers That they had known each other she Mae sufficiently assured. That it was the sight of Conyers that had atrieken Beryl down at the door of gt. Dust's church she was convinced. She believed that the two bad known each other is Switzerland, and that Conyers had made love to Miss Star, and had won her love. She believed even that Conyers might have betrayed Beryl into a mock marriage, Huplick officiating as clergyman; but not one thought of the actual troth penetrated her mind. How could she Barmen that Lord Hawkliurat's heir was actually and levelly married to Beryl Star, and that she, Octavia Windsor, wise not his wife ! Had she suspected the hideous truth, she would never have admitted Beryl into Hawkburst House, would never have schemed to bring her face to face with Conyers, and would have devoted every energy to the task of crushing her rival out of existence. Her suspicions were become to her almost certainty. Tire desire to verify them, and to lay bare Conyers's corset, waa become almost feverish. "I think, Miss Star," she said, as the silence grew oppressive, and Beryl betrayed a sudden rest- legume., " that I will have a set ale* in pale-blue satin, the designs entirely different. Ah I There comes my husband, now !" She arose, her face glowing with triumphant A careless step crossed the dressing-room Boor, the door opened, and with the words of an opera- long upon his Into, Dane Conyers came murdering into the boudoir._</p>
        <p>iTo be continued).</p>
