        <p>'. DEPARTMENT STORES" IN rRE STATES.</p>
        <p>The New York correspondent at tar' Morning Poet," in an article on the attempts of the anthiMpartment Store Party "to restrict by legislation the competition of the Department Stereo with the mall retail traders, writes :--, Early in January,from 20 to 25 of the State Legitimates will he in el anion, and in view of the re-as- mrobling of these laev-making bodies preparations am now being made in New York and in many of the other large cities for a renews! of the legislative attacks on whm are known m the Department Storm. The co- .iwratly. trading movement, nit was long ago demi- -pod in Industrial England by the will operatives and the ironworker,, and mors recently In London by the mmants, has no counterpart in the United States. P. is almost unknown here. It has been tried in • few place.; but American workpeople are too constantly on the name,and are of too many nationalities to permit of the permanent usociation and mutual confidence which are erceesary to the co-operative system. The Depart- ment Store which is now working such greet economic and soci•l changes in all American citiee is like the co. operative movement in England only in the fact that it iiielodse every deacription of retail trade. The beat of Mese American Department Store. are an Mincingly* that if one of them were traneplanted to • city of 20,000 or 30.000 people and all the other shops in the place were Mooed the Department Store would meet neaMy all the needs of the community. These stereo are owned tat:lndividual cepitaliets, or by syndleates with large financial reams.. In every city they occupy the heat Wee for retail trade. They advertise largely and eys.. tematicallg. They neually buy direct from the Ma.. facture, Most of them do only • mob trade, anl in commun.* of these advantage., and of !lie large ecale of their operation., they are able to nil on narrow mar- gins, and they cut price. te such an extent that the ordinary retail trader cannot held oat naiad their men. petition. Them newer methods of retail trade, in their general application, data back to the rally eightiee. Soon after the department Store came into mietence the electric railway came into vogue, and since then tie development of the Department Store hae progressed with the growing network of the electric railways. Every city nf 20,000 or 30,000 people now him ite sya- tem of overhead trolley Manta., by which the suhurbe and the fermis( Mime. for • embus of twelve or fifteen mitee are hr-night into cheap, frequent, and quick con- nection with the city. Every city of over 30,000 ha. also else or mere Department Storm. At lira and before the Mectrie railways were developed, the Department Storm told roost against the ono-line retail trader, in the ctiw. With the development of the railway, they began to draw en the country trade,and they are now playing as much havoc with the country retail Morena they are acing with the onehine otare• of • better class in the eitits. The Department Stores largely monopollee the isteh trade,and leave to the country storekeeper and the onaline retailer only the Peon'e who mtief Mee ,:iog credif, end who cannot mail themselves of the larger stores and of then ewer method, of trading. At masonn like the present, when holiday shopping le being done. Ye Department Stores in New York or Boston are lie great fares. They are %fronded from the time they open until the doors are closed in lb* evening. During the busy hour, crowds collect in the street awaiting • Mance to make their way Into the Mom% and the torn nem at some of the greater storm at Christens time reaches into millions of dollars. In the larger raise there are well-defined gradations in Department Storm One attracts to noel( the better clan trade, endshandles the more expensive lines of rade. To another goes the medium-clam trade, while the management r.f a third eeeks to cater to the cheaper and lower elms demmd. The general plan and method. of all are, however, alike, and the movements for hostile legisla. tine are aimed at these all. The grotto&amp; on which Anti-Department Store enactment, are being sought from the State Legislatures will come as a surprise to Rritiets pmple. In the United States they do ant Dien much surprise, for the underlying principle of all thew proposed State laws is the same as underlies the American Protective meet.. It is meetly themmn ael tie primiple on which the 21101,10 y Act, and later mill the Dingle, Act, were framed. The propmed Anti. Department Store Laws are all haw) on the assumption that people exist foe the benefit of the traders, ano not the tradeee for the COalVeSiaaeo of the people. It is weed againet the Department Storm that they croak out the small trader, that they concentrate ths retail trade is each city I. the hand. of • few people, that</p>
        <p>they cause the geol.l busicess portions of cities to become more and more congested, and that these I ehange• enhance the value of reel sesta in one part lof the city and urcluly depress it in another. It le also urged that by toroth, out of existence the mailer shop- keeper. the number of men who must work for wag.e instead of being in hoodlum on their own account ie increased, and that with the increase In wage-earners there must come &amp;crew, in the rate of wages paid. • farther argument egainst the Department Storm Is that they tempt women to buy articles that they do not need, and that women elsould be protected from the allurements of the bargain counter. The. are acme of the objections which are being made to the de- partment stores In Chicago, and the grounds on which , the Legislature of the State of Illinois has been salted to pars enactment, against them. Almost @gently the same reasons are put forward in every State in 'Mich hoe'ile legislation is being sought. Nor 4 tbe Dope- meet Store movement COIIIIOKI to the bolted. States. Acme, the border in Cenada it is quite as active. Tbe Ontscie Parliament opened its ...ionic; Toronto a couple of weeks ego, and among the Ant petitions prnented to It was one for legislative action againfl the stores. The allegation. again. them In Cicada are that the croon. heti.] of the bulk of the bush.. of • province In the hand, of the owner. of one or two storm is seriously detrimental to the inters.. of the prow. ince at large, sed that the monopoly of 25 or 30 trade, ton mingle firm under one roof creates a fete groat fortunel'and ',Alpert.. the rest of the community It is further argued in the Toronto petition that it I. better to have 1,000 storekeepera fairly prmpernes than two or three millionaires nod 997 bankrupt tradeemen. The movement now on 1000 io New York against the at.e, is at preman shiny in tbe hands et the chemists end:oround,. They want en Act from the State Legislature limiting .he male of 