        <p>A pretty hat for • young lady is of heliotrope hese and main artietically leaved and caught up with sequin bands and loops, between the piquet of four mtrich tips and which retain. them n in different direction. A hat I new the other day looked admirable with folds of velvet roinid the crown, them loosely arranged, and falling well on the brim. Theee folds ehould be started from the left side, and two bias lengths of velvet joined together, end the end arranged in a high cluster, partly on the crown, and finished with a bird and osprey or • tuft of coque's feather. Rosettea 'miler the brim are mule 'loin bias strips of velvet, or of medic or velvet in a Hunter shade Three stripe are gathered at either edge, then folded, and rolled round and rotted to form rosette., making the oentre one larger and the eide ones rather smaller, to fill op the space left by the curved brim.</p>
        <p>Another fashionable hat has the back slightly shallbwer then the front, and the left side is reisco I ho plvmngo and of et if csnvas, ciorixt,l hy velvet , just where the brim is tilted. This trimmed hat hes the crown *overt,' with velvet and drawn op to it oentre hittion, and the velvet here in cut mt the cross, and in shout five inches deep, and the crown is finished by a folded hand of the velvet, enessuring two inches in folds, or five inclice plain.</p>
        <p>An elaborate evening drees has the new lace sleeve. and tho complete overrkirt of lace is a beautiful design. Ao evening Empire dream in pale green silk looke very nice with Empire sash of wide blue velvet etriped with black. Turban of green eilk with border of blue and black velvet., plume of black ostrich feathers or paradise plumes fixed with a jewelled brooch. The ekirt of this drone ehould be very scanty, and the petticoats worn with it very soft and clinging. Oren milk iliockinge and, either green or black satin amnia</p>
        <p>A walking centime suitable for this time of the year hoe the skirt made of eh.. fioent, Wads venetan cloth. Tho bodice of 61.1 to/0..r, dt; cord hes revere, cuff., and collars of chinchilla of the finest quality, while the belt ie made of engraved dull noel joined with large medallions of cut eteel. It is becoming very faallionable to wear mauve and starlet. I cannot commend it, yet I have seen a certain bizarre splendour aohioved in a gay, of torneto-oolour cloth with towhee of poppy-colour shoot it, worn with a fantastic LOTTO entirely of ehaded violet. The wearer was a rich brunette, of rare type ; it is not everyone who eau off .rd to look so blame. The ruby.coloured and brick-red coats and skirts that nne moots in conjunction with violet straw toqtea are quite (Mei&amp; the pale of anything that approa,has to carte, although they are worn often by the weariest folk.</p>
        <p>In the simplest of mulberry cloth gowns I saw a delightful rtf t of ornamentation easily achieved the other day by the addition of a pointed alma- lated yoke of ivory satin stitched on to back and front of the bodice. Theo it was ornamented oith scrollery in a narrow gold and black braid, and the iine where the satin joined the cloth was cun- ningly hidden by the braid, do that the whole looked like. bit of neat applique. The same ides was repeated in narrow pointed cuffs to the sleeve.. It gave • perfectly inexpensive and plein gown. look of the utmost distinction, and one mold not help applying the idea to a purple gown, or to blue.</p>
        <p>sixty large vol- umes, while the impactrtr under his charge more than seven hundred distinct and Separate apartments, to all of which there is a roaster-key. Its ...urn in the way of painting., minatures, drawings, engravings, books and W.S.S. old china. blot sec armour, and arms, carvings, furniture, tapestry. and gold and silver plate. ere unique. The kitchen at Windsor Castle is • most elaborately fitted apartment. It is nearly fifty feet in height, and has an enormous fire at either end, with • system of spite,like university kitchens. As an ordinary etaff,there are • else Jr cuisine, tw master cooks, two yeomen of the mduth, two roasting cooks, two larderers, Ave econrers,nne steam man,and three kitchen maids, bemde lemming men. Every detail of the arrangement* is worked out with the greatest care, and the dishs are handed straight to the footmen by the cooks. At Buckingham Palace,so inconveniently situated are the kitchens that the various &amp;lakes for tha Queen's table have lobe carried on trolley.: and lifts, surrounded by ebarcoal-heated,hot water cupboards. It must be. source of distnem to the cook [het the plats must be brought along 110Cil a labyrinth ol corridors and pas- sage.. At ...enough House the kitchen is extremely accessible. It I. etarhed throueh swinging class doom, and at the farther end is an enormous fireplace for roaat- Mg joints and poultry. An oven is attached to the open Are place,but much of the cooking is done by meene of • wend It is in another apartment that the entities are yr...died the confectionery too has an apartment to it.if. Spacious larder, act to be seen in the admirable London how.. . . .</p>
