        <p>Many of the game larder. belonging to War es where big elle, tine given, require to bent huge dimensions. The game larder at Sandringham will hold six thoueend head ! It is only exceeded by that belonging to the late Baron Hirsch, on his estate in Hungary, which wilt hold nine thousand head. . . .</p>
        <p>.111 r •UrnaltaUal The King of Sweden who is expected to pay a brief I whit to the Queen at °Marne, and the Prinm sari Princes* of Wales at Sandringham next month, is • man el extreme culture and literary attainments. Ho omelet in 113.57 Princess Sophio, • daughter of the Deign of Nassau, sod has proved himself • most devoted husband to his ailing wife. Their Majesties have four MIN hut no daughters. King Oscar arranges his time to the beet pmsible advantage. After an early break- fast with the Queen,he gives audience to public officials, Swedirb, Norwegian, and also foreign ministers, reviews and inspect. troop., and so forth. Friday is occupied by a council meeting, and Tuesday morning is given to the public. The King sees even the poureet peaeant and most unknown being in his kingdom who wishes t speak with trim, and gives audience alone, no third per. iren being pro sent. The evening, and indeed the beet pert of the night, King Wear gives up to his literary pursuits. Sweden is like that conntry which was stud to be happy because it could boast on history, and the same may be mid of its Q men. From the day she made her entrance a. a young bride into Stockholm. Sophia has given up all her life and thoughts to making her hus- band happy, and furthering in every way the good of her people. To her children she has ever been a most devoted mother. Unlike many women of lees exalted *Winn, she considers it extremely wrong to hanp over • ebild to nurses and gt yerneases, and during the yearn which permitted her husband's acceseion to the throne she employed every moment she could spare from her other duties in supervising and directing her young sous' education. For some long time put tow Queen Sophia hi.. .pent • portion of each year abroad, bur she is tnuchtogly remarked "It is better to be ill at home, than well elsewhere." . . .</p>
        <p>Billiard playing among women is steadily on the in. crease. Only a few years ago this was considered essen- tially a male pursuit, but nowadays many of flue sex are extremely skilful in the game. Our own Princesses have always been good players, and bane taken with them to the German Courts and elsewhere, the taste for the pursuit. The Dowager Curies of Russia inn gnod player, Queen blargherita of Italy also delights in wield- ing the cue, and she counts much on the exercise neces- sitated by walking round the table, for diminishing her tendebcy to stoutness</p>
