        <p>Ml unnatnrel s, he fain* soli eidadtophspof W. to gn'tin as and valuables were solicitor returned will," he mid, "sod tog members of the fa Day after day, week afiek mouth rolled by without aiIYN the missing pair. Friends lost earth had opened suddenly and and then clo,ed up again, thk could not have illeCU more complete of the year dawned, brightened \ deepened into twilight, ho, still no</p>
        <p>The day after, the solicitor arri, servants, and shut up the house It now. In this elate it hiss remained one knowing if its owners are alive . This was all that my predeceseor ham fallen to my lot to hear the sequel : One day about twelve years ago, I Bryn Llwyd, for that is the name of when I noticed an old roan wanderings garden. I wondered who he won, anti business there might be, and paused an him. When he observed me, he limited, once came forward, and, strange to say, sal almost the identical question that you di morning. As I told the story be seemed' much affected and kept his face turned from is.. , once I noticed a elicibler pase through him, 81111 when I hail concluded he sighed deeply. 'He had known the vil:age when a boy,' he said, 'and had loncvd to see it again once more before he died.' The nem day I woe sent for hurriedly ; an old nrin, a stranger, had been taken ill close to the gate of Bryn Lloyd. I went at once and found it was the same nein I had been talking to the day Istfore. carried into this house and my OM housekeeper and I nursed him between on. There was no hope from the first, he warn very oil 111111 dreadfully emaciated, and thong, we nursed him with tendernese and care he never gained any etrength,—ths spark of life gradually died out, and one day we buriesi him in our little churchyard by the sea. A few days before he died he relieved his mind of a burden which had weighed it down for year.; he confided to we a secret, mil that secret explained all that will ever be known concerning the fate of the lost Squire and his brother. This was what to. dying man revealed .</p>
        <p>• I am an old man, mieerable, feeble, and dying; who could recognise in me the stalwart young squire, Hugh Gwywdd, who disappeared an many years ago? Neverthelese lam he. For more than forty weary yearn have I wandered over the face of the earth and at last I have come home to the. Let no monument mark my tomb, for I am accureed ; blood is on my hands. Oh, God ! The blood of my dearly loved brother. Can there be forgiveness for a sin like this? Ab, no! I am murderer—a fratricide. Why do you not recoil with horror, and leave me to die, as I have lived, alone!'</p>
        <p>"This unlooked for revelation astounded me, but putting all that on one aide, I net myself to comfort him. •No sin is too great for God's mercy, I said. 'Only repent, and your sin will be forgiven. Who ehall measure the magnitude of sin but God, and He is an all.wise Judge? Why ehould I recoil from on, my brother; are we not both sinner. in God's eight?' The poor invalid laid hie burning tremulous fingers on my hand and said• Let me tell you all. When you have heard everything, if you can still talk to me like that, I shall almost begin to believe in God's mercy, slaw man can be so kind.' His eyes remained clotted for awhile and he seemed to be recalling every. thing to his mind, but at length with a deep heari. broken sigh he continued:—' About forty years ago I was expecting my brother Owen home from India and I had filled the house with people, and we intended to have quite • festive time. Among the guests was a young lady to whom I had become passtonately attached. I had not yet spoken of my feelings to her, but her manner to me caused me to think that my love None returned. In due course my brother arrived, bronzed by the eastern sun. On ! how proud I was of him. But from this time Gwendoline's manner underwent a subtle change, she woe cooler towards me, and avoided being left alone with me. The thought that she was growing weary of me was misery. Loving her devotedly, I could not bear the idea of her shpping away from me, en I determined to put my fortune to the teat. The next evening I found her alone in the censer. vatory, and I poured out my heart to her and told her how I loved her, begging her to try and love me a little in return. I took her hand, and would have kissed it hat she drew it hsetily away and told we that what I wished could never be, that she could never love me. When I realised that I wan merely another addition to her long lint of conquests, spurhed aa no many before me had been, I was filled with anger and mortification. It is impossible to remember all that I said, btu I know that I accused her of luring me on only to throw one over. I was fuming with rage and disappointed love and rushed away after I had hurled the word coquette at her as a Barthian shot. No euspicion of the truth had dawned upon me yet, but the veil wan soon to be torn from my eyes. I could not remain indoors, the atmosphere seemed to stifle me. I put on my hat and rushed out. Not noting the direction I took, I walked quickly until an further progress was etopped by a stone well, which had been put op to keep the sheep from falling over the cliff..</p>
        <p>• A more lonely spot than this does not, I believe, exist upon this lonely cout. The slate cliff. rise sheer out of the water to a diuy height, and far below the breaker. are for ever lashing themselves into foam over the needle.like point, of the rock., until the voter look, like milk. From this spot no habitation is visible, the village lies behind a bend and but for tits sheep dotted about here and there one could imagine oneself the only living creature for miles around. The intense silence and solitude soothed me, and my mind gradually became calmer. Would to God I had gone home then flow much sin and misery might have been spared Her. the poor old squire buried his face in his hands, and I saw the big team slowly find their way between his fiugers and fall upon hi. long white beard. • Between the wall against which I we, leaning,' he continued, • and the edge of the cliffs there wan a narrow strip of turf. it had been a favourite place of mine att a boy, and now placing my feet upon the stones which projectoel from the roughly- built wall, I sprang lightly down upon the other si,le. I just mitseil coming down upon the prostrate tic. r.• of man which lay there ;he at once jump. d up, and I ease it was toy brother Owen. He reugtostal or at the 8111111 e time, and threw him- self onut more upon the ground without saying • word. My astonishment was great to find him here and looking no dejected and out of spirits. • What's the tt °tilde, old fellow 7 ' I said, 411 I Mat down by his sale and threw my arm round his shoulder affectionately. • You re looking quite worried.' Am: I stretched myeelf out on the soft toil and prep ceil to li7ten with the thunder of</p>
        <p>Owen</p>
        <p>e Astonishment kept me dumb at host. Them the most probable cause of my rejected love rushed ever me, and my heart was filled with rage and jealousy. • Perhaps the feelirg is mutual and your honour has come to the fore a trifle too late,' I sneered. 'I have not spoken one word of love to °wend°line,' he said wearily, ' and I don't under. stand what you mean by the feeling being mutual.' • I only mean that you have before you a rejected lover, and that you are the canes.' ' Then that I. all the more reason for my enfant departure ; if she cares for me at all, it in only a panning fancy, and when I am out of the way it will all oome right.' The demon of jet! easy meet have blinded me, for instead of seeing hia noble self- abnegation, I could only see how his eye• had brightened for • moment when I told him I had been rejected. I waa on my feet now, and facing him, saying in tones of concentrated fury, • You shall never marry her, curse you. You vile traitor, to come between mound my love, and then, when It is too late, to talk about your honour.' Then.. • dreadful thought came to my mind, I rid with as oath, 'You want to go sway, do you! Ha! ha eo you shall, and twill see you off.' My arm. were round his body before he grasped my inten- tion, but when it dawned upon him, he struggled and tried to free himself. For a time we wrestled in deadly silence, he giving way inch by inch before my superior weight Hugh, spare we!' berried at length as we neared the edge of the cliffs. Hat murder WDA in my heart.. I answered, • Have you spored me my love? You wanted to go sway, on go.' And with the etrength of • ramose I lifted and threw him.</p>
        <p>'His hands °aught the edge of the cliff, to which he along with a despairing grip, and never, Oh Heaven shall I forget the look he gave me. I rest &amp;se It now, it box never left me, and wherever I look, there are his horror-stricken eyes fixed on etine. Fort moment he hung enspended, and thou, with a ory for help, disappeared from soy sight.</p>
        <p>.It wits only now that holly realised what I had I done, when he was beyond my help. There could be nooloubt that he was dead, death wee the ger. : min into of anyone falling from thwe awful cliff. : I waa • murderer. I had taken the life of my brother whom my mother on her death-bed cont. witted to my care, and this wan how I had fulfilled 'ainztl/4,,,dtrust. y feelings were indescribable. I was filled ).‘ with remorce, and flung myself on the ground, elnce . ' g at the earth in agony. How long lln . there know not, but when my reason returned, night h I fallen. My erat and only idea was flight, a d that as rapidly as possible. Hugh Gwywdri ceust be buried with his brother. The murder, niest take another name. Home and fi Mods musk know me no more, we would perish together. SO I flak 'I made my way to Holyhead, and Item, my Intention to work my passage to America es • loiter. On reaching that town, I bought some coerce sailor clothes and se I allowed my beard to grow, I was soon no altered in appearance, that I might have passed my most intimate friend on the mate footpath without fear of recognition. I boarded in a common lodging bowie, and became. companion of the lowest drege of society. Of course I had changed my name, and I ssaideouely studied the Mannere and talk of milers, that I might more easily pass for one. At last I got a berth, and after a stormy passage, landed in America. It is needless to describe my life during all these Intervening years, suffice it to any it has been on. long history of misery and wretchednem. And now, though I never intended to set foot in Wales again, here tam, come home to die, drawn by an impulee I could not reale. Your great kindness in nursing a forlorn stranger has conquered my resolution, otherwise 00 000 would ever have known that this wreck who lice before you ia Hugh Gwywil 1 of Bryn Llwyd.' So finished the history of this micereble Aniline creature, and a few day. later he left this world of care and sorrow ane entered his eternal rest." Silence filled the room for some minutes after the Vicar had completed the story and then as there was still sufficient light I begged him to show me the gave and the some of the tragedy. We walked out is the still, cool evening air to the little churchyard, the Inmate. of which are lulled to sleep by the never. ceasing murmur of the restlees se. He pointed to a grave, marked with • plain headstone, on which two initials were cut "H. G., nothing more, for it was his dying wink that he should remain unknown even in the grave. We left the grave in silence and walked towards the cliffs; the big waves of the Irish Sea came rolling along until they encountered the rceke, when, bursting with a booming noise, the spray flew so high that It reached us where we stood. "It was hoer," my companion amid, in a hushed voice, and we stood awhile in silence, picturing the whole wane as it had taken place. 'Both brothers are to be pitied, tot how much preferable the fate of the one who died here," I said. "God relit their souls," the Vicar mid fervently acme turned our steps toward his house. We parted at the Vicarage gates, and the next day I left the village. In due course I arrived at home, and settled down to another year's wotk. . .</p>
        <p>Some time afterwards I heard that the kind old man was,dead, Co I may IIOW use the per. MiZaloll he gave me to publish thin sad story on his demerit And I tenet it will intereet my reader, se much on it did me, when I heard it related is %het little outobtbe.world village on the rads. bound mast of Anglevey.</p>
        <p>A BUSH MURDER IN NEW.ZEALAND. The " Melbourne Argo." publishes the following message from its Dunedin correspondentorritten on No- ,ember 15th:—A most shocking munder,similar in many !respects to the murder. of Butler in South Wain, has lust come to light in the Hutt Valley, about nineteen miles front Wellington. Ernest Alfred Hawthorne' a stalwart young manAwenty years of ageovaa engaged in the bush there nutting sleeper.. He hod., mate. man known as Stanhope,abont thirty years of age, but since known to have many aliases, such as Pluipots, Wells, Smith,and Wood. Their hut,thoueh not • great distance • from the roadArae cnmpletelv hidden in the dense bush. ! and was in • very lonely position. Hawthorne may br! maid to have been an exemplary young man, quiet, and hartitorking,and given to savieg.his money. 2astte9 • . .</p>
        <p>recklessly 51 ending. at the public:house. He attended to religious duties, disliked being in debt, and be leg very unassuming, was much liked by the mattered settlers who got to know him. Some ten weeks or so ago Hawthorne disappeared from the district, end Stanhope shifted his ramp. In muster to inquiriee, Stanhope said his mate had gone to Dunedin, and esplained that he sold his tools and camp to him,at the Same time producing • receipt from Hawthorne. The settler, were not convinced that the young man would go away without sayieg • airord,and when Stanhope was arrested onnohnrgr of forgery their distrust was not allayed. The police were informed of the matter, tint their inquiries elicited no information,and they were in- clined to believe Stanhope's story about his mate haying got i away. The march for mme trace of Hawthorne was continued after the police hitt by a settler, who oh- *need that a few yards away from a path witch had been used by Hawthorne and his mate the ground pre- sented the appearance of having been disturbed. He at once communicated with the police, and on the ground being carefully turned over Hawthcrne's body was found. With the body wae found • hatovhich was identi- as Hawthorne's, aed in this wat found a small bole. An examlnntion of the body dimloaed a small wouod on the back of the left side of the head, evidently camas] by • m vole, Wilt. The hole in the bat would corre- spond with the wound in the head if the bat was worn on the trek of thy head. The wound could only have been self-indicted be • lefthanded man, and it was improbable even then. India/diens :how that the murderer shot his victim as they were walking on the path, and then dragged the body a few yard. into the bush. Apparently a start had been made to dig a grave in a gully below tbe I hot occupied by Hawthorne, where the yegotation was very thick, but for some reason this was abandoned,and the hole dug whi re the body lay, every car, being taken to remote all