      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>AFTER MANY YEA</head>
        <p>AFTER MANY YEA</p>
        <p>BY GERTEODZ Wurreatm.</p>
        <p>11 Several year ago whilst on • short holiday Anglesey, I came across one of the meet thoroug Welsh villages I have ever seen. The cottage., more properly speakiug hovels, were all thatched, and !ooked no ph:tore/mile no one could desire. Yet it in not likely I should have made any stay there, had not a striking fmture of the village attrmted my eye, and rouged my curioeity.</p>
        <p>At a little distance from the road, and in ita own grounds, stood • large dilapidated old house, which had the appearance of hosing been untenanted for years. The garden hod no doubt once teen beautiful, hut now was nothing but • tangle of untrimmed bushes and trees. Everything bad • forlorn and neglected air. In the place of the soft springy turf which had once covered the lawn., rank grass and wild flower. flourished; the gravel drlve tree hardly diatiognishable for word.; one half of the gate had meted nfl it. hinges, and by on the ground, while the other hung loosely, creaking dismally es it swung to and fro.</p>
        <p>I strolled in to get a better VilW of the house itself. Ruin and decay were apparent on every hand. Only the roses which had been someone's care and pride in times long peat continued to flourish and seemed to mock, in their beauty and luxuriance, at the frailty of man's handiwork, saucily nodding their sweetdicented blossoms and peeping tent the broken windows. I gathe;red a kw of the finest, and after retracing my steps to the little inn where I was staying I asked my land- lord how it was that so ilo a hone° had been allowed to fall into decay. It was very little that be could tell me—no one had over lived there since he could remember ; but he had heard his father tell how, when he himself was quite • little boy, the squire and hie younger brother had both dis- appeared in one night and had never since been heard of. It was • strange story and seemed wrapped in mystery. Always of a romantic turn of mind, I tried to conjecture what had become of these brothers, and even in my dreams I pivtured them vanishing into space in • weird and myeterious</p>
        <p>The next day found me again wandering mood the deserted gardens. I wished, if Feasible, to enter the hon., and thin I found it easy to accomplish, by the aid of a broken window, which was only about four feet front the ground. As I sprang down into the room, clouds of thick pungent dust disturbed by my entrance, arose, filling my lungs and nostrils and causing me to sneeze and cough violently. When it had somewhat ;Reified and I could see a little clearer, I found myself in a room of large diinensions filled with decayed but hand- lame furniture thick with the untouched dust of years. The spider—its only occupant—bad spun his web in every available place, rejoicing in the abeenee of the broom. I don't know if I half ex- pected to find anything, any clue to the mystery, as I passed from room to room in the ghostly still- ness, opening a drawer here and there, or peering into a cupboard; if I did, I was disappointed, for I found nothing of any importance and I was very glad to be out in the sunshine and sweet, fresh net once more, after I had clambered through the same window which had given me entrance.</p>
        <p>As I made my we; from the garden, I became conscious that I was notalone ; an old man stood lean- ing on the gate and watching me curiously. Wham I came nearer he greeted me in the hearty manner of the Welsh, earl inquired if I was staying in the village. eject for a day or two," I replied. His dress was that of a clergyman, and his accent wee refined and educated. He seemed as if he wished to speak to me, and came round to my aide of the gate "I am surprised I had not heard we had a stranger in the village," he remarked, "Stranger. are scarce with us, you must know, and are valued accordingly." He laughed pleasantly anal said "1 am afraid you will think me very inquisitive, but I should like to know if your visit is due to liminess or pleasure." " Purely pleasure," I replied, "I was walking through your village, when I noticed this old house and stopped to examine it. My landlord at the inn has told me all that is current respecting the disappearance of its owner and his brother, and I feel much interested in the strange story. I. there no one, I wonder, who has the key to the mystery ?" The cheerful tusk upon his face died oat, and a look of sadness took it. place " Why would you thus pry into secrete benied in the past! It is a and story, a sad, sad story." His voice was subdued and his look far away as he murmured Mess words. " Young man, / Mone can tell you the whole of that story in which you say you are interested, yet ahall I? I emit decide lust now, I must think." We walked a few steps m silence, and then he said Come and see me this evening ; I live in the ivy-covered house by the church, and I will consider meanwhile aa to</p>
        <p>whether to impart the secret to you, or That evening found me comfortably ensoonced I n the vicarage dinning-room (I found I had been quite tight in taking the old gentleman for the village clergymsn), and after gme convervation the Vicar salted me if it was gill my with to hesr the.story.of the loot. brothers, because if robe had</p>
        <p>deciderrto relate it. I eagerly assured him that my interest was in no degree lessened, to he settled himself in his mayehair and began as follows "When first I came to this village, it sus ae ',trate to the late Vicar, who was aged and infirm heft was who made me acquainted with the first part of this mut unhappy affair. More recent events which have taken place since I was ap- pointel Vicar, have put ine in poasession of the whole facts. These facts are known to no other person living. Let me first relate toe story as far as my old Vicar knew it : ° Hugh hiwywnld, who was the squire of this little village, and Owen, his younger brother, were the only surviving descendants of an ancient bunily. The squire, who was still • young man, MIN very fond of society and kept hie house con- stantly filled with guests, to make up to him if possible, for the loss of hie brother Owen who had been his conipsesion ever since he eould remember, bat who wu now a soldier, far away in India. So far both • brothers were bachelors, but among . . _ . .</p>
        <p>H—ugh a guests ogre numbered a Colonel and !di; Wynn with their beautiful daughter Gwendoline. to whom the imptire was feet toeing hie heart. But Gwenilnline Wynn wee not going to lose command of her heart to easily, sae wan aceuatomed to be admired and petted and loved ; these thiugo .he seemed isr expect as her right, never thinking whet the consequences might be to others. So Hugh Heed on in a fool a pariaher and the words which would have banished his hopes for ever were not spoken. G encloline had not yet seen the man who could stir her very being, she wearied of them ell in time, and could not believe in thin passionate romantic love, which she had ;lever Bit, but her time wee fast approaching. Owen BO written to say he was coming home at last, and great preparations were being made to receive lUni. One day he arrived looking son- hrowntil and manly, and immediately became the life of •he party—the gay became gayev and eveti the grove became mirthful under his influence. hut at length found her fate ; now she koew what love was, she knew it. yenta- true and its jeolonsi., its sweetniiwe and it. bitter-</p>
        <p>it;ee oeTe..l was gall-tint to her as it was his manlier to he to all ladies, but title was only happy when he was by her side. He treated her oonencbrother might • slater, for Hugh had confided hie intent ions-to hint. But these feeling., there hortee mei feels were hidden beneath the sur- fece, end only known to their respective owners. To the unioitiated, all was going as merrily as the pie...t01d...1 marriage heti, until one evening, when ...Ph the brothers disappeared mysteriously end completely without leaving a trace behind. The moist, assernhled in the drawing.room, woode,c.l whet hid become of their host, and Gwendoline, sitting apart and gazing through the A‘lll,lOW into the feet deepening twilight, woo tilled with vague foreboding. of evil to Owen, lherk neon fell and they had not returned. At last everyone bean to get seriously alarmed, conver- sation was carried on in whispers, people came not went quietly, a nanwleee evil and dreadful relamity seemed no brood over the house. Search antes were organised, and every nook for a con• eitlereble dietence explored by the aid of lanterns. rho eestch continued throughout the night, bat fruitlessly. Morning dawned on groupe of weary</p>
