        <p>In varions part. of the country on Wednesday gather. lags of Liberals were held in honour of the amaiveniary of Mr. Gladsthne's birthday. At the National Liberal Club, Inindon, ouch • meeting took place for the Net . time. The following message from the venerable stale.- ' man was read : " Cann., Christrnateday, 111197 —I think poor appeal to one a great honour, and in reply I heartily with that the coming and every mtrequent meeting map be add dto the purposes of truth, I.e. nice, honour, peace, good faith, and all that is of good report." Mr. F. S. Stevennon, M.P. (who occupied the chair', Mr. J O'Connor Power, Mr. E. J. C. Morton, M.P., nod Mr. Elba Griffith., M.P., were the Wet meeker.. fhe Chairman sail Mr. Gladstone', personal. ity was the pommainn not of • party but of the nation, while Mr. O'Connor Power declared that if they had but one spark of Mr. Gladatone's spirit there wan net one item of the Liberal programme that could not be carried to victory. Sir John Brunner, who bad recently viaited Mr. Gladstone at Cannes, wan the principst speaker at the annual banquet at Cheater. He reported that Mr. Gladstone had green amble eel. dence that he still retained an elamlutely perfect Mot 1, ory, and I.i. ph...chine were very hopeful of relieving him of hi. neuralgic pains. at a dinner at Ripon Lord Ripon spoke _of Mr. Gladstone'. great efforat on behalf of Home Rule, and said the question Mill held the salmi pond°. in Liberal policy as it did when Mr. Oladetoue Tem in office. In regard to the Indian frontier trouble. he mid his remedy would he to make friends with the tribesmen, guaranted their independence, and tell them that if they were attacked we would help them. That would elm ba the policy of Mr. Gladstone. At Hayward.. Castle a mosidereblenumber of congratulatory message. were received, and many more were telegraphed direct te Cannes.</p>
