        <p>extraordinary develop= Icing place on the mast point to a collusion •.of those two power- s which are bound 1 commercial interests, commercial interests of SBURT has been bullied or isormany ill connection with e Greek quotstions to such o DOW universal testimony to !mance of this country was sat of Europe as it is now therefore that both Russia night this to be a favour- enipt to play the same In fact, when the his- s is glanced over one has Ang the belief of Sir JOHN at the birthday banquet at</p>
        <p>Chester on Wednesday, that if there had born a Gladstone government during the last two years this would have been a prouder country than it fa to-day. When composing the message quoted • above, there een be little doubt that ' Mr. GLADSTOU had in his need not only 1 the east of Europe, but also the Indian frontier and South Africa. That special reference to " good faith " can he given a vary direct application to the campaign against the hill tribes of the Hindu Koch that has just come tea conclusion that is practically abortive. This destructive and worse tlmit useless Saar was a direct 'result of a breach of faith of which any 'civilised Government should be heartily ashamed And as to South Africa, how can the words " justice, honour " and " all that is of geed ' report," be made to harmonise with the establish- ment of a shameful system of slavery in Cape Colony. All round, indeed, there has during the Ilast two years Man a very marked degradation I Imperial policy, and there is already a wide. I spread feeling that a return to the sentiments and methods of Mr. GLAINSTON6 would be both 'or the honouo aad the advantage of the country.</p>
        <p>Fr is a true and a trite saying that Aci=tatnit. you cannot please everybody. A huge</p>
        <p>petition, signed by no fewer than 104,388 of her Scottish subjects, has been snit Le Han MAJESTY protesting against the violation of the Treaty of Union between England and Scot- land in 1707 by the common nse of the words England and English, instead of Great Britain and British. But we will let the protesters state their own grievance. They say "This general and continuous use of the terms " England " and "English " in an Imperial sense inn direct aggres- sion on the national honour of Scotland, and in extremely irritating and annoying to all Scotsmen who have regard for the honour of their country. For such usage implies that Scotland is part of or is simply a province of England, and that Scots- men are subjects of England. It is insulting also to our personal honour as Scotsmen ; for it iee plies that so long as we obtain all the material advantages that are the result of a union between the two countries,we are of no mercenary a chant, ter that national honour is to to a matter of the greatmt indifference." It must be confessed that this last insinuation by the Scottish petitioners is a trifle unkind. The famous Dr. JOHNSON, and indeed several of his contemporaries as well, used to say uncomplimentary things about the Scottish invaders, but now-a-days one never hears disagree able inuendoes of that kind. We are all now rodent to believe that the Scots come south solely for the purpose of benefiting England. There is one other comment to make. The petitionern in ventilating their own grievance deliberately ignore the claims of the sister isle. Iriabmen ,would still be left out in the cold if the words Britain and British were aysternatically used, yet Irishmen have done a fair ahare of the work of building up the Empire, mpecially on the battlefield. And while some Scotsmen are com- plaining that they do not receive all the nicogni• tion they deserve, a eergeant of the 3rd :Ride Brigade writes from the Indian frontier to ask if we are all' Scotch mad" in this country. He appears to be of opinion that everybody in England bas turned Scotch because so much fees has been made over the warlike feats of the Gor- don Highlanders, while next to nothing has been said about other equally deserving soldiers. On be whole, the Scottish. petitioners will find it very difficult to convince anybody that their grievance is a serious one. The world in general will, is is to be feared, still go on using the word 'England as synonymous with the British Empire, That is one of the advantagm of being the pre- dominant partner.</p>
        <p>W. SKELTON, LlNtlf ED WAR EINGTON.</p>
