      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>DECEMBER. 31e, am</p>
        <p>Mr. GLADSTONE'S birthday _ was</p>
        <p>again celebrated on Wednes- day with unabated enthusiaam. The veteran statesman has now entered upon his eighty-ninth year but he is still in the enjoyment of an extraordinary amount of mental and physical vigour. At the Is present thee is, in rdanaccoce with his custom during the last few years, "spending the drearieet portion of the winter months in the genial climate of the South of France, but he has ex- pressed the hope that by the middle of February he will be" at work again at Hawarden." At a gathering in honour of the occasion at the National L.beral Club in London a message was read from Mr. GLADSTONE the wording of which shows very clearly that, whilst eschewing merely party controversies, he is stills close student of imperial politics. He expresses the hope that the meeting of ti is year and every subsequent meet- ing "may be addressed to the purposes of truth, justice, honour, peace, good faith, and to all that is of good report." It needs no microscopic reading between the lines to ace that this message has a very direct bearing upon the political situation that has been created by two year's tenure of office by the Tory party, especially in regard te foreign and colonial affairs. It used to be a standing charge against Mr. GLADSTONE by his political opponents, that his foreign policy was devoid of all strength and energy, but it is not at all improbable that by this time some of his moat blatant accusers would much rather have one hour of GLADSTONE than two years of Ssusllcar. No doubt in the course of his career as an administrator Mr. GLADSTONE made his" graceful concessions,- just as Lord Sausanar has done, but then, as the chairman of the London gathering very pointedly remarked, Mr. Gcsnsroxes concessions were made to the weakest and not to the atrongest ceded the lonian</p>
        <p>ththdy.</p>
        <p>foreign powers. When Islands to Greece, he performed a signal act of justice mid at the same time did something towards strengthening a nationality upon which the hopes of freedom in the East largely depend. There could, in connection with that transaction, be no sugges- tion that this country was prepared to give to the powerful what it denied to the weak. In his deal- ings with the strong he displayed no weakness when he thought the just rights of this country were about to be encroached upon. When what was known as the Penjdeb incident on the Afghan frontier threatened to disturb the relations be- tween this country end Russia there was not a shadow of weakness in the measures that were taken to provide for the worst alternative, and his vigorous policy towards th 3 SOLTAN of Turkey in 11380 was the very antithesis to that of Lord SAW- soar in dealing with the same incorrigible mis- creant. Lord SAWS/WRVS concessions have been of a very different character. Be has exhib- ited a compliance that has verged up- on weakness towards the United Staten and Russia, and Germany and France, while there has been little or no hesitation in using the argument of force against those who were, Sr seemed to be, comparatively defencelem. No right-thinking person will condemn conces- sions or friendly bargains of any kind with foreign countriee so long as they lead to • better mutual understanding and a clearing away of possible causes of unfriendlineas. The unfortunate thing about Lord Sausnuar's "give-and-take" arrange- ments, however, is that they are, as a rule, all give on his part and all take on the part ef the other countries concerned. What has been gained by his numerous concemions It is clear that France has not been conciliated, for that country appears to be beat upon making greater demand. upon British good-nature than it hu:ever done</p>
      </div>
