        <p>_ . Ot.o listrunwairla Laws TONTINE Culla In connection with the above club the annual grant hag been made to the members iit 12. ',eh. The total re. mitts during {An year have heels 1.239 91 114. Tne taro oiliture had been £l4l 1.6 1, I.aainq a balance of £95 as 5. 'lll, club w.s ciiinp ndat 164 members.</p>
        <p>During s,. tpinhCiiisent. liunoorm the , Maitland Wool, refene4AW4ll Taisa which the osii had sustained by rie diath of the late Mr. Jam,. Littler. At the comcluelon of the nervice the organist (Mr. Johnsen) pltved the D.iff "March in Saul," the congregation meanwpile Standing. SPZCIAL Mandrel 00 Tile T 1 RH. Nevem, Commit.. A curious{ meeting d Din Runcorn Urban Council was held on Wedneeday morning, in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, Mr. S. Taylor, J. P. CC., presiding. It was decided to draw on the treasurer for the 11111.1 of £5OO in favour of the Board of Agriculture security for the morons en local Inquiry as to regulaticie of</p>
        <p>A floscong-Bomin Vassal, is A Gams. The Norwegian vesiel, i• Praemdent " laden with tire, bee from Alava tieatia to Runcorn experienced very hard weather off the West Coast of Ireland between the 18th and 18th inst. 9 large portion of the cargo was swept overboard. Considerable ifarnage sews also done to the yowl. The vesael arrived in English cetera. few days ago and sti.oprda Eastham :where it was unloaded in- stead of at &amp;meant.</p>
        <p>ST. Eon... Rife 1.1.6 01 TIVI Canes BAND. The above band has just bees re-formed and on Thurs- day evening the members sat down to an excellent Sapper at the Presbytery. Irwell-lanediuncorn, provided by the pastor. the Hee. P.th, de, fire rev. gentle- man presided over the gatheriag, and in the course of his remarks he congrakulat,d them on the re-formanon ofthe band and endrested the hope that they would get many engagements. Games. Psz., were alien indulged in tin a late hour.</p>
        <p>lIIINCORN BOATING Clam, ANNUAL Bac, On Wednesday °wooing Me annual ball in connection with the above club was held in the Public Hall. The hall had been nicely decarated for the occasion by lift.J. Stet le. Tnere were abiut BO persons present.whe danced to the strains of Hr. F. Knight's band until the smell hours of the following morning. Mew.. W.Crosby and F. Chrimes proved efficient Itr.T.Lea provided refreshments.</p>
        <p>A Sends. Audis,. us Tel Suer Cans, On Tnurany inorning the steamship Maggie Si erring. ton, De[oozing to Mr. Samuel Rough, Liverpool, ran into the Wider side of the Skip Canal at the Old Quay, Run- corn. The ship was proceeding from Manchester to London with a general cargo. At the Old Quay the eteering gear rave way, and she ran dam on to the ens- berk men, Ste remained aground for halt an hour,and then continued her voyage, with the intention of calling at Liverpool to rake in more cargo and ascertain the damage. 01{1111,ITION or Tin Rev. J. 0,03,8V15, CA., lianColl, On Thursday evening eel interesting ceremony is an; nounced to take place in the Bethesda Congregational Chapel, High-3door, Runcorn, where the Rev. J. Os- borne has been acceptably working for some months. The Rev. John Yonge,of Warrington, will speak on con- gregational principles, whilst the Rev. Dr. Ferber° will deliver the chard to the pastor. A special sermon I Will also be preached on Sunday the 9th Unwary by the • Rev. le Carter oath, C4ll\ FP to the church and congreg- ation. Omer particulars will be found in another Column.</p>
        <p>Sr. Emituan's CHURCH Cohpectrons' olntrss.l On Tuesday evening the collectors to the number of 18, connected with St. Elward• Church, Uwell-lime, Runcorn, were entertained to dinner by tbs pastor, the Rev. Father Core, at the Presbytery, Owen-lane. Father Oore presided over the proceedings and lathe course of his address thanked the collecters for the work which they had performed daring the year which was just drawing to • close. floe party afterwards indulged in games, kc., and a jolly time was spent, The company separated at !even o'clock, and all area thoroughly pleased with the fun I hey had had.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS or TiNIPERANCR. Mr. Edward Key, jun.. presided over the half yearly meeting hold in thii Pruning's, miscolroom, on 'Thursday evening lass. After the meal reports had been submitted and socounta paned, the following officers were elected for the ensuing sir months :—Patriarch (chairman) Mr. Edward Key; Associate, John Thrones Fiswldes ;Conduc- tors, Messrs. William Edgeley and Malcolm Jones ; Sentinel. Mr. John Cook ; Chaplain. Mr. Thomas Whyte; Financial Secretary, Mr. Edward Key, son. ; Recording Secretary, Mr. Thomas J. Molt ; Treasurer, Mr, Daniel Eisnrown ; Sick Stewards, Mesas. James Jones and Thames Clarke; Auditors. Messrs. Thomas Cooke and William Co ke ;Trueees.Messrs.Themaslieemmahornm Whyte, and Thames Clarke ; Siicretary of Cadets, Mr. James Johnsen ; Patron, Mr. Thomas Whyte ; Masers. Thomas Beeson, Thomas Clarke, and Malcolm Jones, The following were selected to repremnt the division at the annual meeting of the Cheater° Grand Division : Messrs. Edward Key, jun., Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas Clarke, Thomas Whyte, Malcolm Jos., and Thomas Cooke, with Mr. Thomas Eleesom to represent the Cadet Section.</p>
        <p>PRtaNNTATION TO MR. JORN !UNCOMIC RUNCORN. The animal 'social" in connection with Mr.Nancollie society clue at the Brunswick Wmleyan school was held on Monday evening. The room had been very tastefully decorated by the member. of the committee appointed to carry out the arrangements, via., M . W. Loath. wood, Bisbrown, Coventry, Hancollis, Johnson,and 3.5 Pickthall. Over 40 invitations had been tont Oct Co young ladies, and co...pent!, there were altogether 80 persons who sat dowo to partake of tee provided by Mr.fiencoilie (leader). After tea the usual RartONl Wel* indulged in, Mr. E. Danien acting as Master of Cere- monies. The chief event of the evening,., a preeenta- tion of two splendidly bound volumes to McNancollie a mark of the esteem and respect in which heir held by the members of hie clam. The pleasing duty of pre- senting the books devolved upon Mr. W. Leatbwood. Tole gentleman in the comae of a short speech said he was very pleased on behalf of the entire °lane to per- form the duty allotted to bine. He believed It, was speaking the sentiments of he brother mew- l:ow. when he said they had derived considerable benefit from the strict training of their teacher. fie expressed the hope that they would experience the mote in the coming year. He then handed the books to Mr. Nencollis amid loud applauee. Mr. Nancollis in reply thanked the members of his class for their gift. ad mid it am • pleasure to him to know that they had appreciate I and bad taken to heart the little advice be had given them at their meetiogs each Bandal. Songs ware afterward• given by Mrame.Johnson awl Bisbrowa, and readings by J. J. Pickthall and A. Dodd. A MOW enjoyable ensuing was speot at the company broke up about eleven o'clock. The Cooke were —Pictorial Cabinet Marvels, and Pictorial Record. of Remarkable Events. The inscription M the hooks was't Presented tour. John Nancollis by the members oPffn nociety clan tea marls of eetevo anti ...pea. W. Coventry seeretary. December 27th, 1897."</p>
        <p>. wands paraded the town an,. yawning. There was no almond town beyond the services at the The football match which shoal St. Helens was cancelled. Igo.. match here, andedneettiehtfy the ealMortera -- papalai parttime had to take up mem other means of amending their TO or 80 minutes which would have otherwim beau spent on the football field. At St. Elward's Catholic Church, four services were held. The first rases was at nine o'clock, the second at ten, and dm third at eleven, benediction being given imnaeliately after the eleven o'clock mass. The Rev. Father Gore mu. the officiating priest at all the services. At the nine o'clock mass about 30 children made their first communion. After the mass they assembled in the Presbytery and partook of a good broakfast. They after. wards went to the eleven o'clock service,and afterwards nuts, sweet, kl.,were diatributed t them. At the last mass the choir under the conductor .hip of Mr. Dockrey rendered as the offertory piece, the Christmas hymn " Adeste Fidelis." The church mid star were profusely decorated with evergreens, Sm. The chief attraction in the church was the elm or the repre- eentation tithe birth of Christ. Tar exterior waa like a stable covered with greens, whits) Mid le were the infant Jesus lying on straw, and also the Blessed Virgin , and St. Joaeph. No pains had been spared in making I the arrangement lock ae real as possible, and Mr. Bishop, who hat the erection of it ahead, is to be complimen- ted on his taste. OP Christman morning Miss Hodgson, schmlinistress ' 01St. Elward's School, dlstributed at the pries&amp; house to ka deserving families, joints of matt.. aa Wedges. : day, before the holidays, a number of poor children ' *Mending the Catholic schools were supplied with clothing. At St. Paul's on Christmas Day a KIMoe was held at eleven o'clock, the Rev. C. W. L. Chrestien occupying the pulpit. At Balton-road the Rev. R. J. Clifton preached. At the Camden Church. the Rev. J. A. B. Malvern preached an appropriate sermon. On Panday the pulpit in St. Paul'. Chapel was occupied in the morning by the Rev. R. J. Clifton, and in the evening by the her. C. W. L. Christien. The choir under the coaductorship of Mr. Ben Crosby gangs torn. her of Christmas c mole. iminediately after the eveoing service, a meeting wee hold at which all the new mem- bers of the church who had jollied it during the last year were present. The her. C. W. L. Christino delivered an addrem. At the Prinsitiva Methodist alhool-rooin on Christmas night, a most enjoyable entertainment waa given in !connection with the Primitive Methodist Chapel. Mr. Wilson presided over a largo salience and delivered a stirring addreed Subsequently the following contrib. , ted what proved to be a aucceesful .I.IiCAI programme I —Misses Hillidge, L. flitchen, Nellie Teraperley, and Florrie HilLand Messrs. J. Rabbis, Pelgrarn. &amp;gimbal!, Woodward, F. Shingler, aud T. H. Ashley (Widnes). Miss L trospeelep placed a •iolln note in a skilful meaner and received • leen-deserved ercore. The mholars, who had been carefully traiued be Ilan Wilson !gave an action song and &amp; scar f drill, which were well received. Mr. W. (Mounds was an efficie. accompanist. During an interval Rev. W. Fidoe gave a practical address. At the Parish Church a choral celebration of the Holy , Cooll.lliolll was held, and at the evening service carols were sung by the choir. The interior of the church was nicely decorated. Oa Sunday a epeeist MM. IVu, preached at the , Primitive Methodist Chapel be the pastor, the Rev. Wil. liana Fidoe from the teat " Peace, goodwill to men." During the service special ChiatEriall anthemn were ream ! dered by the choir, Mim Wilson taking the solp. A col- lection was taken on behalf of the trust fonds. At St. John's Presbyterian Church, Victerimmad. a special service was held on Sunday evening. The pastor the Rev. Sidney MorDs preached en appropriate sermon from the words " For auto us a child is born." The choir rendered Christmas carols, and mama! choruses Dern the Messiah. Mrc. Timporley sang a solo entitled I know my Redeemer liveth," from the Messiah. hilts C. Pritchard gave" Bethlehem's ions "in • pleasing manner. Miss Warburton and Miss Bonne Harrop also sang e Nola each. Mr. J. E. Warburton cmducted and Mr. J. Richardson was the commie. DUTTON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>On Christmas Day the interior of "The Rotten" pre- eented quite • gay and festive appearance, several por- tions of it,netably the dining hall and chapel, being de- corated in • manner b fitting the oneatioa Breakfast, served at the unual houagras followed by a short service in the chapel. This included several carolawell rendered by the choir. Dinner was partaken of at mid-asY, and cuetomary (by C. kindness of tht Gutrdiane) eondsted of the good old English fare of roast beef and plum pudding. A pint of beer wae also included for each of the adultathe children being supplied with milk. The repast was followed by it distribution of orange, to all, and of" the fragrant weed " to the votaries of "My Lady Nicotine." Before separating h.rty cheers were given for the Guardians and the officers of the house. The supper It II rang at 6 o'clock, the good thinen served up for Me meal including tea coffee, buns, bread and butter, currant and read cake. At intervels during the day the young- sters, muih to their delight, were regaled with sweets and oranges. As an agreeable termination to the day's festivities a 'short entertainment was organised, when the Minter (Mr. Cutinali) presided. The contributors to the programme included both officers sad inmate., the juvenile element largely predominating. The fol- lowing inn list of the ladies and gentlemen who contri- Wad to the enjoyment of the iniuttee : —Miss Beat (Anton), oranges ; Mn. Wright (Balton). oranges and sweet&amp; ; Mre. Rodger (Newton), hound volumes of " British Workman," " Cottager and latillan," Child's Companion," and "The Friendly Visitor" ; ?di. Elsie Wright (Halt.), Cheintenna cards ; Mimes Geeenall (Walton), parieh magazines for the year; Mre. Whitley (Stretton),buns; Miss Rigby (floylake). Christmas cards; Mrs. Mclntyre (Bud worth i, book. ; Mr. Best (A,t,m), tobacco and Christmas card. ; Rea L D. Lyon Bennett (Little Leigh). evergreens ; Mese.. Parkinson and Son (Warrington), toys.</p>
