        <p>ari;r7ly be In ...it similar happy position. I think I shall be expressing the unanimous feeling mot only of my good friends of tbe District Coun- cil of Runcorn,but of all its people,and I make bold to add of the inhabitants of the neighbouring towns also,when I say that it to not Sainte that Runcorn should remain for • day longer than is necessary in this invidious and solitary position. With these feelings in my mind,at the close of this Jubilee yesr,l ask you to receive herewith on behalf of the District Council my cheque tor 000, partly in fulfilment of my premise already named end • partly to pay off the remaining debt, Pray convey • to your Connell my good wishes for Christmas, and for the New Tear so noon to begin.—l remain, dear Mr. Marshall, yours faithfully (Signed) Pm; T. BRUNNER. R. Marshall, Rig. A meeting of the Technical Instruction Commit- tee was held on Tuesday night, Mr. J. Wilson in the chair. The committee decided to recommend to the Council that the recognition of Sir John Brunner'a generosity should take the form of a ban. quet at which a piece of plate should be presented to him.</p>
        <p>At an entertainment given in the Primitive Methodist school, Greenaway-road on Christmas , night, the chairman was Mr. J. Wilson, Chairman of the Runcorn Technical Instruction Committee and he referred to Sir John Stunner's generosity. He said he had some Rood tidings for them that night, news which concerned every ratepayer and young person in the town. Some time ago Sir J. T. Brun- ner,the member for the di vision.promised the town a further donation of £5OO to the funds of the Tech- nical Institute on the condition that the town raieed £5OO. This would have paid of £l,OOO and Nit a debt et £4OO. They had in various ways given and begged the £5OO on their part, in order to obtain that promised by Sir John Brunner. Mr. Marshall sent Sir John word that they had raised the amount and instead of him sending them Silk, he had actually sent • chepue for £9OO, thus freeing the Institute entirely of debt and removing a block from the pathway of educational progress which ' hindered them much. He (the chairman) should like this repeated in eYery cottage sad home and workshop throughout the town. It had come at • very opportune time, slime of goodwill to men, and was done not for Sir John's personal benelit,but for the benefit of the young life of the town, both of this generation and of future generations,and he deserved the gratitude of every individual for his timely gift. (Loud applause.)</p>
