        <p>PRA. 0. MI. E. Dusum, Roncoa..</p>
        <p>It is with great regret that we have to record the death of Miss E. Duguid, late teacher at Granville-at. Board whool,Runcorn. The deceased came to Kulsoorn • little over twelve month, ego, from her birthplace, Aberdeen,Scotland. Intending to go home in order to Spend Christmas with her parent., and also with the in- tention of And.. • school somewhat nearer home, She tendered her resignation to the authorities connected with the school. Os Priday,the day of her departure, it mu found that she was ton unwell to pyrunt of • long ioarney rain,and it was decided to postpone it • day later. TO. t ,00ptonts of the diastase—which proved to he diphtheria in its most malignant form—rapillv de- wehmed,and serious eornplicatioes ensued,which termin- ated !stall, the d, (—owed young lady pawing away at 1 5500 Wednewlar morning. Mi.. Dugutd,who has for Some tire. Ismn living at Mts. Janions, lord-street, had during her Stay in time town,gained the esteem and re. apt of P•OVV,In she had come in contact with, She had already passed, and had been summate', in several begotten, ea nincions,and 1.11 talented to • high de. ire., despite hoe youth, her ego being only II yam. Much sympathy is felt for the aorrowing relatives, the sad blow coming melt has,at such a j•iyous festive name. The remelts; of the dec....used Miss Helen Doguld were noveyed by hearse Ppm. on Thureday to the rail. way station and then, to Aberrieen,Ma Llverpoolaby the midnight esprosa Before the c irtege proceeded on its way to the station the Rev. Mr. Christien (superintend- ent of the IC ealeyau Cicero) read in tte- parlour of the deceard'a late residence, a heautiful and iinpresaive Chapter out of RMelations, while the friends of the dm ceased gathered outside and sting In a beautiful the hr um beginning. Th., is a lend of pure delight." The mrnmful prmeasion then wended its way to the station, and when assembled snag over the ooffla "Shall we meet beyond the river " Among the mourners and friends were Mr. W. Millington, Mr. Plekthall, Mr. Knivetne. Mr. Alfred Dodd, Mr. 1. Smith, Mr. W.Jones, Rev. Mr. Chtistien, Dr. McLennan and Mr. Janlea, • few net of • greet Ther were aemeral beech. ful wreaths, illoatrative of the high esteems in which she was NIA. Atm Duruid's sister travelled with the collia from 'Liverpool to Scotland.</p>
