      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>INQUEST AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>INQUEST AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE CORONZRT RI!tROSITECT:</p>
        <p>On Monday, We pace veto Informed of the sudden death of John Shoe, who was found dead In hod that morning in his house, Woter-etrwe The docelsed went totted on Sunday sight about 1030. and appeared all right. About 11.311 new morning hit daughter celled him, and receiving no reply she west into his room and found he was dead. 1 Mr. J. B. Worshoy, coroner, held on inqueot on the • Only, on Wedneedoy morning at the Police Station, Bowan. Mary Burke, said deceased was her father, and was about 55 years of age. OR Soft .0 he appeared In his daunt health and went to bed. Neat morning she coiled him but receiviag no wearer she went into Inc rows and found him dead. Dr. Robinson said he remembered the particulars of the law Mews which demand hid twelve month* ago. He wan then on the point of death and the Catholic Church odmiuistored to him the lot sacrament. He was not sting from • lung affection. He followed the employment of • stone breaker and this nod übt mfr.. ted his 1.1601. His lung were prontically done. The dint from the stones would cows the lung affection. He attributed death to chronic lung disease. The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the walical testimony. Before onparating, Mr. W. Wright (formon of the jury),a, behalf of hit fellow juryman wished the coroner the compliments of the Reason and emir...wed the hope that thee 1•0,1111 not see him as often as they had done that yew in Runcorn. Mr. Wortley, is reply, thanked them for their kindly espeeeelon. He said he had been the coroner of that district over 25 yews. Since that time the population had greatly increased, hat he wee glad to wy that there were fewer inquests in the year that waS just drawing to • clew than there were when he was appointed • quarter a • century age. He Pet it down to the fact that the people were more temperate it their Tobita than they had Wen in wars gone by. He did not memo that the people were teetotal, ha they were mote sober In their White. Mr. Horsley referred to Runcorn jurymen and enit he had never hod occasion to reprimand • Run. corn juryman since he first sat ast coroner. The jury- imen of Runcorn wore more attentive to their duty than any others.</p>
        <p>In Chins the hen Is kept nOnstantly busy. When not engaged in hatching her own brood ohm is enamelled to hatch Ash egg, !he spawn of ash are placeal in an egg- shell, which is herrnebcally sealed and planed under the unouspeeting hen. Alter some day. the eggshell is re- , moved and caret silly broken. and the spawn, which has been warmed into la e, Is emptied into • shallow p well warmed by the aun. Here the minnows Ufa develop are oni.l until strong enough to be turned Into a lake or stream.</p>
