      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWs</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWs</p>
        <p>February, 1868, wee in one way the most wonderful month in the world's history. It had no fell moon. January bad had two full moone and so had March ; but February had none. This remarkable state of things had waver happened before since the era we assign to the creation.</p>
        <p>It may, parhapi, not be generally known that there it at least one country town in England which cannot boast of • mayor and corporation. This lithe ancient and interesting capital of Buckinghamshire. In the diya of Queen Ntiamaylesbury was g anted • charter,but by some means subsequently got into disfavour in high guarMrs and net it. The inhabitantn have of late real. ised the indignity of their position, sod are tusking a strenuous effort to llcelebrate the Jubilee hy getting a new charter. They do not want a renewal of the old one, becauss it provided for offici ila known as o ale tasters," and some of the inembors on the presont local council are total abstainers.</p>
        <p>Bendigo, at which Mr. Sroddart's eleven has just played a niatch against eighteen nt Bendigo and district, is the f amous Austrelian goldfield on which the Msrquis of Salisbury (then Lord Robert Cecil) had • tr-ste of dig- ging Ste in 1852. Bendmo rejnises in the deepest geld iniees In the world. It is practically a series of gold- , fiel oiled nn OP of each other. In seine of the mines ' the local millionaire. Mr. Eleorec [Ansel!, good payable 'gold is being raised from a depth of threrequarters ot • roils. The national garne has alsonys ft mri,hed in Ben- dign, which hen produced two of the greatest Austradian cricketers ever seen on English lielde—Bnyle and Mid- winter. The lett, had the unique distinction of touring through England neon Australian cricketer, and of terming through Anetrailia as • member of Shaw and Shrewsbury's English Eleven.</p>
        <p>' Scientific lectures in board and other day schools on the effects of aleohel on the human system is another development of the desire to protect the young from the insidious effects of drink. The colonies,however, have shot for ahead of the mother wintry in this matter. Dr. Clifford,the eminent Bapthit mioister,of Westbaurne- groire,London, just returned from. visit to the colonic% has been telling us what he has seen and heard in Canada and Australia respecting temperance teachiag in day schools,which has caused some of us to open our eyes. The Education Minister for Manitoba take, high ground,and lays down the following propositions Saber habits are more important to a man than a knowledge of arith- metic, grammar, or ether subjects taught in schools." it Sober habits depend, largely upon. knowledge of the injurious effects of alcohol upon the human system.. "Therefore,. definite place should be given in the school time-table for instruction aa to the nature and dna, of alcohol ; a reasonable amount of time should be allotted to it, and the standing of the pupil should be estimated according to his examination in this subject."</p>
        <p>Althre.gh the great Are at King's Lynn was happily I unattended by any lose of life,it might very cagily have proved a terrible climes to the long series of Christmia. tragedies. The deaths by Are of ten persons at Bethnal Green, of nine at Chicago, and of five more at Deptford Dublin, Duedes, Berwick, and Middlesbrough repreeent • awn of suffering °ensnared with which the destruction of shops and booms to the value of £50,000 Milkt seem a alight calamity. But the escape of sick people, women, nod children from the homing cottage. was little short of Plif4ol,ooll. It now appears that the toisn'e Are estineuishing appliances were hopelessly inadegnate and ; inefficient. The steam ere engine bribe down at once I and there was nothing with which to Arbt the games but taro small manual engines until heln come from Peterboro.ek and March by Train lii. to be feared that many small towns ore.. badly provided in this reapect as Lynn, but their danger does not appear until it is Plr. posed by • great en Lunation. Good may therefore tome out of evil if the lesson plainly taught on Monday Is not neglected and if the typhoid stricken cottages of this old East Aeolian town are replaced by better and eatthier dyr ellinge for the poor, the great Are of iLynn, like that of London It Hee, may prove merely. disguised blaming.</p>
