        <p>Mr. A. J. JOU.ay said he wished to thank the lomaten for the very hearty manner in which they hod jolted In the expression of thanks by °haring the Guardian. who had proYided the dinner which they had to much en- joyed—.at Heat he hoped they had dons so. Fle had to spolog•se for the aterace of the Chairman of his Board. who had vent to say that he mould not attend. Tema- ally he (Mr. Jolley) wished to thank the officers, from the Master and Matron downwarde, without whose ex- ertions the dinner would not have been the somasa it had been. It want a great deal of work for many ample, but all the thoußht and work n quired bid, he felt sure, been cheerlully given. They also wished It thank the Mayor and Mayoress for their presence. In con-lusion, Mr. Jolley wished all present • lderry Chrietra. ad a Happy New Year." The MAT., who was received with applause, said it had afforded the Mayoress and himeelf moon pleasure re attend. He felt sure that the inmates owed • deep debt of gratitude to the Guardiat for providing them with such an excellent meal, and they no doubt felt grateful. As • townernan,and as Mayor of the town, hr considered that thanks were due to the Guardians for the are and attention they took of the inmate., end he was pleased to tee them no happy and to comfortable in the ciremmtancee Ho proposed that • hearty vote of thank. be Riven to the Guardiant for their generosity. (Applanse,) Mr. Monate, an behalf of the Guardians, thanked them for thew appreciation of what had been done. The ,ster and Matrundt should be statei,had had an all- otos Comas well an thae who had assisted them, and their servicee shoold also be recomileed. (&amp;pplaused The Rev. H. V. PIOOT said he would take advantsee of that opportunity of echoing to what had been ..it ehout the debt ef gratitude they owed to thy Master and Matron and the other nfficera for the treat that they had provided for the inmates under the order. of the Guar- dia.. . . . .</p>
