      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>STEAMER SUNK IN MORECAMBE</head>
        <p>A STEAMER SUNK IN MORECAMBE</p>
        <p>EXCITING SCENE. TWO RUNCORN MEN IN THE BOAT.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday there arrived at Fleetwood the captain and creme of the steamer Bebe, of Liverpool, which founded in Morecambe Bay during thsfinight of Tondo, The Robe, which bad a crew of six all told. under Cap- tain 'Frovrimend, of Ulverstno, wee en a voyage fro n Whitelviven to Ellesmere Port, with a cargo of iron-ore. When near Morecambe Bay lightehip heavy weather ma. experienceJ, and huge seas were shipped, which broke the aft c onpani a, smashed the skylight., and flooded the alter hold and nabin, The water penetrated from the cabin to the engine-Tonto where the engineers, after a vain ettempt to keep siiriam up, were driven Imre their poste. The vessel became unresongeable, and it was keen that she was about to sink, the darkness of the night added Co the peril of the eituation. Eventually the crew found it necessary Co take to the boat. Morecambe Light, bore W.N .V 1 ,about five miles off, and Blackpool ma. doe: emit, about ten mile.,. The wind was W.S.W. With the ntmoat difficulty the boat was at last launched, and now began • struggle for life, which lasted the night through and well into the following moraine. It was eine o'clock when the boat put off, and not Are minutes later the Bebe went down in fifteen fathoms of water. Theorem's most strenuous efforts were directed to keep the boat &amp;lost, and it was only by continuous and unto- - "</p>
        <p>- . . witting baling that they succeeded in do. I hog w. For sin hours—till three in the morning—the ileht with the sea and their own growing weakness was maintained till finally, all but dead,a landing was made on the beach at Bispharn. There they made their way tea farm—Mro. Gregson's— where every attention was shown them, and later in the day,after communicating with the owner of the steamer the men went on to Fleetwood,whsre they were sepplied with s complete rig-out by Mr. It. C. Ward. ths lomat agent tor the Shipwrecked Mariners' Society, the whole ot ths crew having lost every article belonging to there. The names of the in, n are :—Captain Townsend, Rivet.. ton ; J. F. ferretta, Runcorn, mate ; R. Reardon, Rim- cornfind 3.0. Sullivan, Liverpool, filet and second co- ok:leers ;W,llia,o Vicars, Dalton-in. Furness, and filbert Jones, Wigan, able seamen. . . . .</p>
        <p>Oro representative called at the house of MyTerretta, Egerton-et., yesterday morning, and had the following narrative of the disaster 'old to him he that gentleman. He mid Toe tame of the boat was "fiche,' of Liver- pool, owned by Mr. Rowlands, shipowner, of Runcorn. They left shout 1.-30 p.m on Tueetay. with cargo of 150 'ions of iron nre, to be unehipped at Ellen. mere Fort. The crew consisted of Captain Towneend, Ulverston ; J. F. Terretta,Rencorn, mate ; R. Dearden, Runcorn, and J. 0. Sullivan, Liverpool, first and mooed engineers William Vicare,Dalton-in-Furnessand Albert Jones, Wigan, able seaman. Shortly after leaving a gale was elperienced, the wind blowing fiercely W.S.W. The crew battled bravely with the storm until they were flys miles E.S.E., ef Morecambe Bay Light- ship. Hare tbe bums seas swept the deck and washed away the aft companion and the skylight.. The boat then ...awed to ell very rapidly. The water rushed into the eneine rooms end completely put out the Errs. The night was dark and to toe the word, of MyTerretta " one could not Sr. arm's length before him " Th. men were very cool and did not seem to realise their position. Signal, of diatrees were gives torches burnt, and a gun met off several timer, but despite all this no one CILTP to their aid. A vessel passed by some distance away,and the distr., signals were sent into the air mon rapidly In order that the crew on the passing boat would amp to ti*r assistance, 'out they evidently did not hear, and soon disappeared out of sight, The only chance now left to the crew of the illtated Bebe was to take to their boat. This they did, and a few minutes afterwards nothing more wan seen of the vessel. The Ma! were raging at their blehest, and no sooner had the men got into the email boat than an aceident befel it, two planks being staved in. With the six men who were wet through and. completely exhausted, the boat eras completely pocked. The men did not lose courage and made what little headway that was possible under the circumstances. Two of the crew had to take up the poets a haling out,whilst the otbers made strews- one efforts to keep her &amp;that. From nine &amp;clout on Tuesday night when the Liebe went down until three o'clock on Wedneetley morning, themes were left to the mercy of the waves On arriving on the beach at Ms phase thee were hospitably received by Mrs Ore./von, Black Farm. They were afterwards provided with clothing and sent horse at the expense of the Shipwreck, FiehernsenX and mariner's Society. The Runcorn men arrived home on Wednesday. Our reporter .1113 saw Mr. Dearden, who Hem at 11, Clarence-street, Runcorn, and both men eeem none the worm for their unpleasant ex. perience on the wild soa at Morecambe.</p>
        <p>0107107 RED FOR LIFE" it the despaiting OCT a thomands afittotad with unsightly humours of thl skin, scalp. arid hair. Do you realise what this dilleuratlon means to sensitive souls ; It mean. Isolation. ..dun.. Ills • bar to social sod business 111.0001 R. Do you woa- d.. that despair seises upon these sufferers when doc- tors fail, standard remediet fail, and noltrums prove worse thisa melee.? Skin hum met are met ohstmate to cure or even relieve. It Is an easy matter to claim to cure thens,but quite another thing to do on. Ctrocang . remedim bare earned the right to be called t. Shin Specific, becalm for years they have met with most yea I markable .I.IICOMIC There see caws they cannot cure, but they are few Indoe4. 111. ne long drawnent ex- pensive experiment. A warm bath wRIt Cpricoa• S..ar and • single anointing with (lunette, pureit of ernole ante and greaten .1 akin cum, ill prove sorer,,. vincing lithe severe,, forma of Itching. burning. Igoe. ing, scaly, crusted, atd pimply humours, with loss of hair, Una pnin of printed nutior.</p>
