        <p>Christmas Day. The baracks r'ci.o.;;;;;;;;;;;;;;; beautifully decorated, and in the evening, when they were CI tilled with dancer.. the scene was one to be re- membered. Etch company had its share of musician. and arranged Es own dance, except in one cam, where two companies had to amalgamate owing to the num- bees being small. All app,red to enjoy themselves to the full( and this Chriatinas will be long remembered.. one of the pleasantest ever spent at the Estracks. CHRISIALSB AY BILL CLIFFE BAPPIAP CHAPEL. . .</p>
        <p>The ancient custom f holding an early service at Hill Cliffs Chapel on Christmas morning was csitinued . thin year, • good congregation assembling at sin o'clisph. The preacher was Mr. James Kenworthy, of Manchester College, and the service was hearty and in- . terrat-ng.—On Sunday morning the pastor,the Hey. J.S. Hugh-, °reached • sp,icial Christina. 'ern,. en "The Son of Man."</p>
        <p>-TIM ;11-tinl treat to the Sunday school wan given on Monday and proved eminently encased!. The children mustered for tea soon alter to r o'clock, bring- ing with them parent, and friends in ouch numbers that It threeraned a famine: however, all immediate want. were supplied. Over 200 must have taken tea. After tea the children's entertainment was held, when the Inland wee crowded to it. utmost capacity. Fare could not be ten. than 250 preient. The paetor was able to take the chair during the earlier part of the meeting, after which the senior superintendent. Mr. W. H. Bradford provided. The Secretary,Mt. Ernest Cirounds,preeented an encouraeing report, shining that for the fourth year in succeseioa the echo ml is making stead, and when... tial progress. After a few introductory words from the Chairman,Master Herbert Vernon Hughes recited an ori- ginal prologue, recounting the principal events of the school for the yeer,in excellent style. Other recitations were given by Misses Ciasey Fleetwood, Catherine Cole. Arne Corbetr, Annie Howard, Elizabeth Grounds, and Masters Lawrence Keewatin, Howard Dunn. .I,ha dances Vinrtall, ;W. H. Grounds, Peter More, and Ben Moss. Music was supplied by Mies Ada ant Mestere John and Percy Grounde,filiss Ethel and Master Arthur Dunn. Mr. Jos Grounds, Mr. Ernest Grounds, Mies Jane Cook and Master Willie and Was Eliza. both Grande. Mire Rooney Grounds gave a violin solo boa very tasteful manner. The Chairman,ide F. Harris, and Mr. E. H. Jones distributed anaa to the scholars, and rivet addresses were delivered by Maas. W. H. Bradford, John Grounds, James Henworthy,and William Arnold. Messrs. A. E. Dunn and E Enrland contra. Hated readings. The soh Rol was neatly decorated. The ioteresting and highly successful gathering was brought to • close by thanks beinz accorded to the ladiee,decora- tors, speakers and entertainers, on the motion of Mr. William Moss, seconded by Mr. P. Cartwright. PArtGaTE INDUSTRIAL SCFIOOL.9. The festival.n in connection with Christmas Day passed off very satisfactorily Sc far as the above schools were concerned. Both officern and children were ...tie early making preparations for the day. The bras band under the superintendence of Mr. York was nut in the early morning. Chy childen attended the Christmas</p>
        <p>. -- services in church, the Protestants at Padgate and the Catholics at Martinscrott. An excellent dinner was pro- vided,through the kindness of the Guardians,which con- sisted of roast beef, vegetables, and plum pudding. There was • good supply of nuts, oranges, sweets, and apples, which the children seemed thoroughly to appre- ciate. The schoolroom wee gaily decorated, with ever. gretna and mottos of various descriptions, which was done by the Matron end officers, who 1/01,0 assisted by the children. There mere present 153 children. In the evening various games were indulged in and all the officers joined, helping the children to enjoy them- selves. The visitors during the day were (—Mr. W. Houghton, Rev. J. J. Langham (chaplain), Mr. Mrs.and !dim Hickman, Mrs. Langharn, Miss Sanders, and Mr. Houk.</p>
