        <head>ENOIN HERS' LOCHOU P.</head>
        <p>THE ENOIN HERS' LOCHOU P.</p>
        <p>THE TRUCE AT AN END.</p>
        <p>The Federation of Engineering and Shipbuilding Ems ployers held a meeting on Thursday in York to conaider the result of the ballot of the workmen upon the terms drawn up at the Conference in London on D .cernber 17. The workmen rejected the terms. The meeting reedved to °endorse the action of its neprmentatives in the Cons Ferenc.," and agreed that " the present hones of labour Cannot be shortened." Approval was expreseed of the arrangements agreed to at the Conference. These, it was added; whilethev afford erorloyer. the freedom which Is necessary to manage their business, do not in any way interfere with the proper functions of trade union.," It Was further decided to inform the representative, of the trade unions that the truce " wider which additional hock-out notices were mispendei during the aittings of the Conference is sow at an end." A Watement arr• made nn Thursday by Colonel Dyer on behalf of the Employers' Federation. It Allege. that 0 no prolongation f the dispute can rffrict their position with regard to the condition, of freedom in the management of their works and the hours of labour. Tinder these circumstances the Federated Employers In. rite the serious attention of the workmen concerned to ths fact that there end tions of management will afford gm opportunity to the individual workmen to earn much higher wages ; that any man desiring to improve hi. position will have opportunities of acquiring greater skill,and that every man will have the right to Ming ant grievance to the direct notice of his employer either individually or through him union." A manifesto, Mimed by the repiesontati ree of mnre than forty trade unions or branches of union, was issued from blanches. ter On Thursday. These repienentativea declare that the terms of the employers are incompatible with trade union principles. They urge the members of the earls mu trade unions to acre to make weekly levies in sups port of the locked-out workmen. The Fabian Society Mao issued an app.t on tv half of the workmen on Thursday eight. A tots of E 10,0,1 wit On Thursday forwarded to the Amide-mat,' Association of Operative ' Cotton Spinners. The Derbyshire Miner.' &amp;modal. ' have forwarded the snide, an additional MO.</p>
        <p>WARIHNOSON BRANCH PONTYRN'S Panknarron. The ninth annual tea meeting in connection with the above branch took place on Monday night in St. Paul's Schoolroom, when about 130 postmen with their wives, friend., sad sweethearts net down to an excellent tea at 4.30, Presided over by Men- dames Sllam, Readett, Griffiths, Johnson, Wrench, Bibby, Hewitt, and Oxley. After tea a short enter. tainment and concert took place when the follow- ing sedate" rendered their services to make the evening enjoyable t—Song, Mr. Bibby ; recitation, Mr. illoodfellew ; wing, Mr. Reynolds; song, Miss William.; song, Mr. Went (Postale/met); song, Mr Wallington ; song. Mr. Leicester, who well earned hie encore. Singing over, the Postmaster (Mr. West), who wow roceiv.,l with chaste, gave a abort speech in much he said how pleaaed he wan to be amongst them that evening. It was, he said, grati- fying to know that dnring this Chriatmas preamure everyone, from the imminent telegraph boy to the highest official in the office, though taxed to the ut- most had dope their duty manfully and, he hoped everyone present would have a very happy new year. After ante of thanks to the artistry, pro- posed by Mr. 1111 am, seconded by Mr. Readett.danc- ing and other gem swere kept up till 12 o'clhek. Asnoriget the visitors who came in during the even- ing were Mr. Mellor (inspector of pow tnen; and t pi. Mellor, and Mr. Broedhurat (chief clerk). The . . . . . _ .</p>
