        <p>Sir Christopher Furness Woe on Wedneaday evening unanimotmly chosen lay the York Liberal Four }limited as their candidate for the representation of the city, and a general meeting of the party will be held to confirm their *election. Lori Charles Bamford has ac- cepted the invitation to become the Unioniet candidate. Mr. Herbert Holman. a barrister, who stated that he had an income of £3.000 A year, was charged at a Lon- don polio. court on wednmday with attempting to atn two shilling.. Mr. Holman, it appears went to • place of entertainment, and afterwards demanded his money back, on the ground that there was no seating accom- modation available. This demand being refused, he is alleged to have steempted to get pnistssi,m of a two- piece, hie reason being, as he explained to the court that he thought if he had the coin he would boll nine-tenths of the argument as to whether he should have his money back or not. The magistrate described his conduct as fonliah, but said ho did not for • moment believe Mr. Holman wanted to steal the money, and I. would be diecharged.</p>
        <p>M. Nobel's will,erhich Yrs; proved on Wednesday,is a curious piece of eccentricity. de I the greeter part of his fortans to form a fund the interest of which is to be annually divided into tee equal prizes. Three of these prizes are for the greatest diecovety or invention during the year in medicine, chemistry, ant natural philmophy reepectively. American miliionaire's often, and European millionaires now and then, have left vast sllo3ll for the endowment of go,lliness and good learning but the peculiarity of M. Nobe.'s Ores is that they are not restricted for conipetition to this or that meat of learning. hut are thrown open to all the world. The fourth prize is ts be given to the most eztellent liter- ary work e. in an idealistic direction "--s, etre.* pretend Ibis to be made by the inventor of dynamite agisinet literary materialism—and the fifth prize into go to the candidate 't who has worked most or best far the fraternimtion of the nations and for the abolition or diminution of standing armiee,se aleo for the promotion and propagation of peace.” Thu. bet prin. is to be awarded by a committee of five chosen by the liorwee /61M Parliament. It is quite safe to prophesy that it will be more difficult to itrafa t nen all the rest. Will It go to • preacher like Tolstoi,or to the politician who has courageto resin, some outburet of Jingoiern.:o the odes. Plc man who by PUMA froth discovery adds to the ter- rors and therefore to the unpopularity of war, or to the royalist like Z or ttoe painter like Verestchagin who sae most trip hfullc represent its horrors P</p>
