      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>TZMPURANCII. LANTESM Lzervioci.</head>
        <p>TZMPURANCII. LANTESM Lzervioci.</p>
        <p>The eitcond of • eerie. of lantern addremes under the anspicee of the Warrington Temperance Feder- ation was held in the Co-operative Hall, Winwick- street, On Sunday evening. There was • very good attendance. Quartermaster Sergeant T. Mantle occupied the chair, and &amp;livered an appropriate introductory addrem. Mr. Taylor, Polies Court miationary, displayed a number of intereeting picturea,thust rating the evil efficient strong drink. On leaving the hall, the audience cam lying in the gutter, facieg the entrance, • man in an utterly ,helplea4 drunken condition, quite unable to more. ' and had it not been for the kindness of SOW nf the. who had been to the meeting some accident must have befallen him. YOUNG MRS'S CHAPMAN ASSOCIATION P.S.A.</p>
        <p>An open service was held in the Lecture hall of the Y.M.C.A. Prince' Green, on Sunday afternoon. Thereon was will filled, and the esr•ice was a musical ene, consisting of a number of old English prelim tunes rendered by a epecial choir Mr. B. Rams was chairman, and gave an appropriate ad- dress., and blearegnon led the devotions. The ser• vice throughout was most interesting and instruc- tive, the verities hymns being well ming by the choir, and in addition Mr. Bens gave • brief den. criptiou of Iho old tune. Gil the development of I the new, which added ronsiderebly to the success of whet wee • roost unique meeting. Mr. Frank Hickman and Mr.G, llollord were. as usual,efficient accompaniers. The past year'. work of the society be. been very succeseful, and the members have kept up their regulsr attendance in a praiseworthy !manner. The numbers have increased and the society is lull of life and vigour, and hopes Sr. high that the coming year's work will be more successful than ever.</p>
        <p>Another of the above series of concerts was even in Parr Hall,Warrinyron, on Christmas eve, but the enterprise proved to be a mistaken one a, the pub- lic attended in very small numbers. The concert however wan good throughout, so that those who did attend were by no means disappointed. The ertistes wore—soprano, Miss Jennie Pritchard; con- tralto, Madame Emilie Young: tenor, Mr. Walter Lewley ; tries Miss Ethel Wane (violin), Mies Edith Wane ('oelho), Miss Kate Bidet, (piano); illusion- , let and •entrilequist, Profesaor Weber, and Mr. Lin- don Wye, t, society entertainer. The Missies Wane and Kidson are very accomplished performers, sod mere heard to advantage in each item, and Mies , Eidson proved herself a clever accompanist. Miss Pritchard has a well-trained voice, and Madame Young sang effectively in "Angus Macdonald.'' Mr. Lawley, who has an excellent tenor voice,gaye a One interpretation of the popular sang from the "Gondoliers," "Tako a pair of sparkling eyes," and receive.' well mented ayylcuce Mr. Lindon Wyatt ,• was a great Recce, Ile was particularly happy in I the musical aksuch entitled "The Band," in which I he gave nary smart imitations of the soiled* pro- duced by musical I.I.I.MBSCS. Profeasor Weber only appeared in the oecond pert of the programme in which he introduced his ventnloquial aketch with life sized figures, and gave an =tieing per. , formulae.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>THR P.AHR FULL POPULAR CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>THR P.AHR FULL POPULAR CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>CHURCH DittC6B.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a euccessful dance in con- nection with the enurch of the Sacred Heart, Liverpool-rood, was hold in Pm Hall, under the patronage of is Wor..hip the Mayor and Mayon (Alderman and Mrs, Wright). A.00.3t 250 persons put in an appearance. The hall wee prettily decor- ated for the occaoion with bunting and Chinese lantern., and preeented a very pleasing effect. Dancing commenced at eight o'clock,and all seemed anxious to got to work The Mayor had to retire early on in the evening in consequence of a revere cold. The duty of WC. fell to the lot of Mr. Oibbins and with the as A AA once of. few others he arrsnmd the dances admireblv. Dancing wm kept up with vigour until • late hour, to the strains of Mr. J. T. Barton', excellent band. The object of the gather- ing was to Caine load, for the new school building, and there is no doubt that the efforte of the committee proved a MCC., —ln connection with Holy Trinity Church, a dance Ins held in the Aasembly Roonn at the Con tire Club. Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening, and wan an unqualified euccem. Dancing commenced at eight o'clock, and wan kept up with spirit until two o'clock on the followin... morning, to excellent music. eupplied by Mr. Yercy Randall', band. A very attic-lent 11.0 was found in the pereon of Mr. W. J. Forebear, end amongst othars present (alto- gether about 100) there were the Rm. If. C. B. and Mrs. Carleton the li41•. H. R. Will, Mr. ldcOnsgor, Mine Pierpoint (Sc. Aniline). Mn. Thomal Welaby, and 11r. Dunbavin. The catering wee in the hands of Mr. and Mrs, George Wetkins, manager and men- agereu of the club.</p>
