        <p>hire. Butler, who otbeen far nearly 30 years leader of the anti-regulation inoyement, alike in England and on the Continent et Europe, accepted • taw ycara ago the poeition of Bulawintetuleot 01 Oh. Polity Dvartinent this world•WiAP organisation. In that capacity she wrota to the Toronto Convention an important letter waying—•• If there is any spirit of cenipw mire in that groat Ariniatloo, or if the election hi in the bands of any perverted r Inn undecided leaden or commute., then I feel I rouat at once sever my cunnecti a with them and you. . .</p>
        <p>The delegate. as.embled at Toronto pained • strong and pointed rivolution, condemning regolarion in any harm. On • previous day the Esecutive with whow it appears the election of officer, rest., re-elected fn. Butler as Superintendent of the Purity Department, but neutralised the effect of her appointment, a. well as the Ire•oletioo ol the Convention, by re-electing Lady Henry</p>
        <p>Or lansing of UMSO-dr-oCeedinan Mrs. Butler wrote to Mies Willard, the Peed lent, on 29M November, reeign• ing the position ot superintendent, inmmuch m accept anee of re-election wuuld be compromising to herself.' : and totter principled for which she basso long... Mended.: This action tommeuded itself to all who have been in :Mom immolation with Mrs. Botier io the war against j redulation and at meeting.just held of the Executive ' Committee of the Federation for the abolition of State regulation of vice, the following resolution woe unanim- Gordy pseud 'That this Committee remoictf Lilly offers to its leader, Mee. Josephine Butler, If. cordial thanks for, and app.,. , elation of, the prompt and eonsietent manner In which at • critical time in the history of our abolition move- ment, she bee reeigned her connection with the World's Women'. Clitietian Temperance Union as World'. Super. intendent of it. Purity Department. : •• This Committee recognise, with gratitude the loyalty to our came expreeaed in the resolutioae unani. j lamely pared by the members of the W.W.C.T.U. at Toronto, but it regards the action of the Executive Com- mittee of tbat body (which has for one of It. objects the promotion of social purity) in re.appointhig Lady Henry Somerset, • pronounced mgulationist, as confusing the public motiment, and as injurious to the progren of the great principle, of this Committee, of which principle. Mrs. Butler hu for nearly 30 years been the chief expo- nent and defender all over the world."</p>
        <p>And this Committee expresses its determination to continue the most uncompromising warfare against regulation in all.finnaltV under every name and by</p>
        <p>-1;;;;;;IZI .iii;irtki;re—who oppose State regulation do ao because it it immcralmnjoet, and against the best in- termits of the army itself;and a point out that where it has existed it has increased vice ad disease. Disease hu largely decreased lath, arnre in England since its abolition. We hold that the only way of permanently d.reasing disease In the Indian army is by getting rid permanently of inch a systeen,and tre arming into ac- tive effect measures which shall diminish the vice itself, many nf which have been planed before the Indian Oaernment by the Federation Committee, and Kim. of which have been since recommended in the reeent re- cent remarkable circular iseued by the Commadereits Chief . Similar Trews have been repeated for many year. by the highest military hygienic authority, the Arm, Sanitary Commission. which has frequently en- preend its opinion that regulation ha been a lailure,and its belief that'' the best practical method of diminish- ing these diseases into be found in establiehing a system of potato, tech hoepitals,and in providing tire soldiers se far.. possible with healthy occupation and recrea- : Son." I may conclude by saying that it is precisely be- cause they abandon there principles, and embody • re- turn to the regulation system, whose results, pl ysically no lea than morally,have been so disastrous,that we de- nounce the leading features of the new cantonment rules in Inoio. lately sanctioned by the Secretary of State, coupled with the repeal of thy Act of 1895 (which prohi- bited the system of regulation there), ss being catrary to the true interests of morality and higiece.—l am, your. obediently, "`"•""'</p>
