        <p>Ma. Jason, effors of the police In unravelling the mys- tery have been of no:avail,the opposite bag been:the ens with the other senution.which arises in Meanie,, a village, sttuate in the midst of Overton Ebbs. Entre. solely itsdred,the circumstances in this instance are far from tragic, yet the mystery which existed remained unexplamed uMil a few days ago. The enquiries et • reporter on the Mart of the r War- rington and Mid-Cheshire Examiner," elicited farts which have for some time interested greatly the inhabi- tants of Alvanley.and should therefore be well wvith the attention of the general public. Residing in Manley-road. Alvanley, is • Mr. John Dugdale, and it was for the pull:pone of interviewing thie gentleman that the reporter made this village hie destination It was not the easiest of plum to eet to,and when once there, Mr. John Dugdale—our Mr. Dugdale— wss act the euiest of persona to find. This is not be- cause he is unknown ! oh no ! out for the simple reason that •Ivanley, though small, bnaete of three John Dug- dales The second whom the pressman called upon, however. moved to be the auhject of nue sketch. Fle Is 57 years of age, hale and hearty, and wean a look of honest geniality which at once Ando him friends. He was both willing and pleased to give publicity to hie recent suffering., and their miraculous cure. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>" About six ;Oaths back," be said, "I arm taken with bronchitis, influenza, and pain. in my limbo, and had to go to bed. My limbs felt am if they were being cut up, and I was an stiff that I couldn't more. The doctor told me I should never be different and had better keep on the elub Every four hours I had linseed poultices on." ''lrour chest was bad, was ill' interpolated the re- porter. ••Awful,''answeredMr.Ougdale with emphasio,"l have kicked all over that lwvi Aghtinz for breath. After three wee.l.:2 got up. and lingered abour.." . .</p>
        <p>"What cured you, Mr. Dued,lt?" was the next query. " Why, Dr. Williams Pink PilLe for Pale People ! My daught-q read *bout them in a paper and asked me to try • box. I did. and before it wri• half empty I felt mach better. When the box was fini,hed I was as well .as now. Everybody who meeta tie i,nnrprieed, end I tell them all, ill. owing to De. William,' pills. They have done a lot of good about here." " You are not the only one cured here then?" •• No, not by many a one." Here Mr. Dugdale gave ease additional information of the good Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had done in elvanley, which he vouched for and which was corroborated by his daughter. Re said "There is • girl living about here who haa been ill II or II yeses. and almor • skeleton. She begot to take the pills when I did, and you would not know her now. She reckono eke inns well now ao ever she ww In her life, and le going on taking then,. She has paid many • pound for Doctor'? Before the reporter left, Mr. Dogdsle added that he was quite at for work again, and at the aimn time gave permission for his photograph to be printed. in conjunc- tion with his statement, in the newspaper. .• I'll do anything," he said t, let pe /pie know what's cured</p>
        <p>WEDDING AT _LATCHIPORD.</p>
        <p>At the Baptist Uhepel, Latchford, on Chriatmea Day, the wedding of Mr. Frederick Grounda, of Stockton Heath, to Miss Ellen Holt, eldest daugh• ter of Mr. J. Holt, of Scott-str -et, Battershy-lane, Warrington, was solemnized. The Rey. J. F. Mat- thews, (putor), officiated. The bride and brides- maids each carried beautiful bcquets,and presented a pretty epectacle. They were charmingly attired the bride looking especially handsome. She wan given away by her brother, Mr. John Holt. Me.?. W. Grounds noted as best man. Mint En Grounds and Miss A. Holt were lb. bridesmaids. Alter the ceremony, and the signing of the register, the happy couple left the chapel amid showers of rice and coed wishes for their future haopinees. The fet- tivities were kept up at the bridegroesn'a residence in Stockton Heath. The happy pair have been the recipients of many valuable preaents, including one from the young men'. Bible class of which the bridegroom was • member.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone on Tuesday completed his nth year. As must on his birthday, ennieratuations are being showered upon him by political and ..thee friends. Lord Kosebery and the other officers of the ficuttish Liberal Club have sent • letter to the venerable statesman in which they sty that his life has been • hrilli•nt part of the history of his country and of humanity. Mr stone, stay at Cannes ho. had iho rifeitof restoring hi. health, and he sr ibis that he espect• to be tt heck Ii wok at Ilawardes "about the middle of February.</p>
