        <head>HARLESTOWN AN I) NEWTON'</head>
        <p>HARLESTOWN AN I) NEWTON'</p>
        <p>COSIGHNOATIONAL CMURCII,.NSW2ON.</p>
        <p>At the cioee of the mini niont-idy free and open I service held on Sunday at the above piece of wor- I An,. aererall selections from the• Slessiah " wore rendered Ins very effective manner by the choir. Collection wee made in sid of the choir funds. P.S.A.</p>
        <p>- The usual weekly meeting on Sunday afternoon In the Co-operative Mall, wan presided ore, by Mr. Willisranon and there was • very fair attendance. An interesting address was given by Mr. W. Gosling and several selections were given by the choir.</p>
        <p>Sr. Jomes CHOReS 9sEcom..</p>
        <p>_ Oa Chrietmas ere the opimbers of this choir turned out with the laudable intention of raising funds for the relief of Mr. W. Nokes one of their number who has been laid suite by sickness for several weeks They visited several places end sang suitable hymns, the result of their efforts at the close being coOsidered satisfactory.</p>
        <p>MOSIC•L EXAMINATION. Al the examinations in practical music held by the Incorporated Society of Musicians at the Royal. Institution.Colguitt-street, Liverpool, the following pupils of Mr. E. Latimer, A 11.0.0., were success- ful in pianoforte playing —lntermediate Grade Miss Mary F. Gough ; elementary grade, Miss Beatrice Jackson. and Miss Ethel Guttridge ; pre- liminary grade, (honours), Miss Ellen Coombs.</p>
        <p>MAnrceiterea CITY Ifneton.</p>
        <p>On the afternoon ot Christmas Day about TO of the children who attend the Sunday echool con. nected with the Marlestown branch of the shore: Mission, of which Mr. G. Dring is the energetic . local leader,were entertained to tea in the Co-opera- tive Hall, towards which the Hon. T. W. Legh, M P. contributed 21 Is. The tea was prepared by Mrs. Dring and the elder scholsrs and it was thorosghly enjoyed by the youngsters. After tea the evening wee spent in games of various kinds, and at In- ter•als songs and recitations were given by friend. of the cause.</p>
        <p>CONSIMVATIVS CLUB, EABLIDITCYN. The distribution of the prizes gained isiTe I usual Christmas handicap at billiards, APO q and whist in connection with the &amp;window Conser- vative Club, took place on Thursday even* in the large billiard room. 'fr. C. B. F. Bore presided and made the distribution. The prima oueistod of geese, turkeys, ducks, hares, rabbits, tine,, spirits and other seasonable commodities. ;ghost of which had been given by tradesmen and skpporters of the Conaervati re. cause. TIIM HOLIDAYS AT EARLSSIOWN ,AIID Newton. Seasonable weather ushered in the festive season and as • consequence a large number of people were early astir. Christmas eve was marked by the crowded state of the ;streets. and the tempting dis- play made by the tradesmen ; who till a late hour appeared to do a brisk business. The Viaduct brim band was out early, and until nearly midnight dis- coursed appropriate music 'n various parts of the town. Several choirs were also out, but between midnight and daybreak, the enrol singers were con- spicuous by their absence, and thus the birth of an- other Christmas Day was remarkable by its extreme quietude. The Viaduct band was again astir early and until noon played several selections at their usual places of call. A large number of people had left the town the preceding day, and on Christmas Day the streets wore a quite and peaceful aspect. The services at the varioue places of worship were well attended, and the sacred °dittosa in several cases were prettily decorated with holly and sea- sonable devices. The Barlestown Football Club afforded an after dinner dixersion for their follow- ers by the playing of a match with Darwen Villa, but the gate was by no means a large one. AS even- ing closed in sounds of merriment proceedi n.l from the numerous residences denoted that festive gat- herings were being held, and the winter holiday celebrated in the usual fashion. With the exception of the Vulcan Foundry all the works were closed on Boxing Day, ant consequently many people Journeyed by train to Liverpool. Manchester. St. Helens, and other places,the attraction being prin- cipally the League match at Liverpool and the theatres.</p>
