      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The remits of the two matehen at Warrington during the Christina/ boliday•—• lon and • win—were not en- tirely satisfactory, but still, after all, it is only typical of the performances of the Werrington team throughout the present season. tif the seventeen matches played in the If utharo Union Competition, only seventeen points have been gained, which represents • point per match, or just one half of what was obtainable. Therefore if we have gained two p Ants out of a poisible four this Christmastide we must rest and be thankful for small mercies. Ths defeat by Widnes on Christmas Day by twenty polars to three cannot be said to be a true index of the merits or demerits of the respective teams. In the first place. Mr. Hancock, of liuncorn, who Ira, selected as referee when the gentleman appointed did not put in an appearance, failed to get the teams thoroughly in hand, and a great deal of offside play oa the part of the Widnes forwards went unpunishe I. Then again, the diVIliBSlll of Naylor be the referee for • in At trivial offence, and the injury Hot., weakened the hone team considerably in the see ,nd half, and thoroughly disorganised it. That Widnes deserved to win on the afternoca's play, few will deny, and it is doebtful whether any team in the Northern Union would have withstood the endoughts of such a wild, harumecarum, rushing lot of forwards as those from Widnes on Christma• Day.</p>
        <p>If such play as that which Widneo gave for the delec- tation of the large pumper of epectators on the Wilder. spool enclosure lithe outcernn of the new rut., then many will wish for the return of the old. The constant each of the Widnee forwards at tip.tap speed, and the chargiog on the opposieg banks may be an effec- tive way of gaining points in the League Comp.tition, but it certainly does not add to the attractiveness a the game, and it into be feared that if it became the general rule of play amongst Rugby club., the gates would grow beantifullr less. In the cue of Wideee, it ia of course rendered more effecti•e by the tall kicking of flatey whose lefty kicks gave the forwards a 8 great an oppor- tenity of getting poswesion as their opponents. Forte, Maly, Warrington a Ime out of the ordeal with only one man Injured, which is something upon which the other home players at least may congratulate themselves</p>
        <p>--- The best exhibitions of thy instah ware given by the two fall backs—Hatey and Beano*. Roth player. were in fine forriLlod their kicking mass testate of the game. EACh kicked with Judgment, but 1331COIV'S kick. were not followed up with that dash by the forwards which characteriaed the Widneeians efforts. The home three- quarter line was superior hi the visiting quartette, rirt to Warrington men were unable to show as much coin. billed play afi usual. lo Hallam, {canine. have • ciri pall&amp; three,' mart, and he filled the vacancy caueed be Harberla injury,with credit :dike to himself and the club. Deanne the fact that he had played at Widnes earlier in the day he showed no signs of ff agging,rini the only try scored by Warrington was largely due, to his efforts. Hereto seldom failed in catching the ball, but he still has nweobatae to bold it too long when running, and any opening the wing man might have. Ishii, wood and Moores play a clever game together, and it is to be regretted that they are not • few stones heavier. Bate and Duckworth are a couple of half backs equal to any in Lancashire, and played a far better game than the Widnes halves—Murphy and Dangles. To the Wid• one forwanie,who went like Zebras from start to finish, must ha given the credit of winning tlin match, flie home forwards worked desperately hard, but they were outpaced.</p>
        <p>The match with St. Helena on Moao arm a very raw affair when compared with that of the previous Saturday. There was very little sting in the attack of the St. Helens men And the score of eight points to nil, does not fully reprivent the superiority of the Warrington team. Barber once *gain amieted his side, and the ovation which he received on entering the field war evidence of hie popularity with the spectators. He still limps • little, and cannot be said to bane fully recovered from the nasty twitch of the knee which he received at Oldham. He, however, pot in some apeedy runs, but did riot receive that usistalice from Heesom which was nee:emery in getting quick ponies. Scott who flied Bates position at half-neck, played a good game, and has considerably improved sits. his Mat appear- ance in the Bret team. Both seta of forward, worked hard, but the Warrington men held the upper-hand, and in It., - ..inages, heeled out splendidly. Special men- tion should be nude of Mason, who played • sterling game. He is strong and speedy, and may be put dowo a. the beat recruit Warrington have booked this season. He was picked out as • promising player in last year'. inter-works competitions when he played with Messrs. Ryland* men. BM Promotion has been rapid, and some good work may be expected of him in the future. armther promising player he Duokworth who is being succeref ally, initiated Into the mysteries and jugglery of the half-back divleion by Bate.</p>
        <p>Of the St Selena men D 'hefty played with more disah than perhaps the rest, although he was ovenhaidowed by Barber. The three-quarter line aa • whole was toe slow in its movements to get dangerous, and Doherty and Traynor were the only two who showed first-clue form. Foulkes at full back etopped weeral dangerous rushee, but Blecow made fewer mistakes, and on the day's play waa the hest of the tare. Briars played • strong game at half back. The forwards were over- matched, and were unable to carry any scrimmages by applying the" screw."</p>
        <p>The Norther. Union—at Malt the I,antaiihire section of it—is vuting • very imperious body. The Warring- ton Cmimittee have been ordered to post HOP warning notices.' all over the town "—whatever that may mean —in consequence at • report made by Mr. Hancock, of , Ronnorn, charging the Warrington spectators with on- ruly conduct at the Widoee match. Now thole who at- tended the match neither saw nor beard anything which could be called unruly. There wstof soiree,, great deal of ereitement,and lot the referee's decisions were rmented by both WM., and Warrington spectators, es- ;windy by the farmer in the early part of the match. Thin is not an unusual occurrence at football matchoe, ▪ not refereed can Mabry, and yet the Warrington .pectatore have but reported as being unruly. It would be interesting to know how the referee came to distin- guish between Warrington spectators and thous from Widaes,and whet conduct ho charaMeriwa as unruly. It was aim on the report of 11r. Hancock that Naylor has been suspended for • month. There arm some surprise stunner the spectator. who. Naylor was ordered off the Held, but it transpires that he ventured to espies. an opinion on the capacity of the referee—not to that gentleman in potion, but to a fellow player. Thing. have certainly come to • pretty pass when • player cannot to this. There WWI • very decided opition amongst the spectators no the referee'. decisions, and few will care tome hits again on the Wildempiol ciente.</p>
        <p>0 the other niatchm Its the latetashire competition doring the Chroitman holidays, the greaten! aurpriae of all win the defeat of Runcorn by Stockport. tin Ott inclined to remark "Row bath the mighty I fallen !'' The number of defeats experieuead by Roh- -1 corn recently must have been downright disheartening Iv, the to titters of the Cheshire club. The tears teem, to have got completely disorganised, but it is to be how, thiir the players will pull themselves together in the:New Year, and etiell again show their true form. To-day Warringtoe liTumitt"--y to Leigh to play off the return engagement. Ths remit of the initial encounter this means liras a dravr,and there was than very little to choose between the teams. The Leigh conunittee have been arrafring raind the dlatrict for good players and from all appearances they mem to bans foiled one in Mohawk, t rem Ituncorn, who playa • aiever half-back eninr,snd sons + a)nbaat overy match. Warringtois havo Anne much bet, tin the eampetition matches, but there such • clori•.l3l nucertitioty about form in tootball, that it Is filth oil to legemet the result. A still' ttruggle</p>
